/* ---------- ratio ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.ratio = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to compare numbers using a ratio, divide a quantity in a given ratio, solve proportions and problems on direct and inverse proportionality, and use a scale to convert centimeters on a map into kilometers on the ground.",
  explain: "<p>How do you compare two numbers? You can find out by how much one is bigger than the other (by subtraction), or you can find out <b>how many times</b> bigger it is (by division). The second way is called a ratio. It's needed everywhere: in recipes, on maps, in problems about price and work.</p>"
  + "<h3>The ratio of two numbers</h3>"
  + "<p>The <b>ratio</b> of numbers a and b is the quotient a : b (b is not equal to zero). It shows two things: <b>how many times</b> a is bigger than b, and <b>what part</b> a makes up of b. For example, a class has 12 boys and 18 girls. The ratio of boys to girls: 12 : 18 = {2|3}. So the boys make up {2|3} of the number of girls. And the ratio of girls to boys: 18 : 12 = {3|2} = 1.5 — there are 1.5 times as many girls.</p>"
  + "<p>A ratio doesn't change if both numbers are multiplied or divided by the same number (other than zero): 12 : 18 = 6 : 9 = 2 : 3. So ratios can be reduced just like fractions. If the quantities are measured in different units, first convert them to the same ones: the ratio of 2 m to 50 cm is 200 : 50 = 4.</p>"
  + "<p>Percentages are also a ratio: 25% is the same as the ratio 25 : 100, that is, {1|4}. You studied percentages in detail in another topic — here it's enough to remember this connection.</p>"
  + "<h3>Dividing a number in a given ratio</h3>"
  + "<p>Let's divide 60 rubles between two friends in the ratio 2 : 3. This means: for every 2 parts of one, there are 3 parts of the other. The total number of parts is 2 + 3 = 5. One part: 60 : 5 = 12 rubles. The first will get 2 · 12 = 24 rubles, the second — 3 · 12 = 36 rubles. Check: 24 + 36 = 60, and 24 : 36 = 2 : 3.</p>"
  + "<h3>Proportion and its basic property</h3>"
  + "<p>A <b>proportion</b> is an equality of two ratios, for example 3 : 4 = 9 : 12. Watch how this works:</p>"
  + "<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_PROPORTION_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<div class=\"note\"><span class=\"hand\">Remember</span>The basic property of a proportion: <b>the product of the outer terms equals the product of the middle terms</b>. In the proportion 3 : 4 = 9 : 12 we have 3 · 12 = 36 and 4 · 9 = 36. This property also works in reverse: if the products are equal, the proportion is true.</div>"
  + "<p>How do you find an unknown term? Let 3 : 4 = x : 12. Here x is a middle term. It equals the product of the outer terms divided by the other middle term: x = 3 · 12 : 4 = 9. If an outer term is unknown, it equals the product of the middle terms divided by the other outer term: in the proportion x : 6 = 14 : 21 we get x = 6 · 14 : 21 = 4.</p>"
  + "<h3>Direct proportionality</h3>"
  + "<p>Quantities are directly proportional if, when one increases by several times, the other increases by the same number of times. Price and quantity: 3 kg of apples cost 240 rubles. How much do 5 kg cost? Let's set up a proportion: 3 : 5 = 240 : x, so x = 5 · 240 : 3 = 400 rubles. You can also do it through the price of one kilogram: 240 : 3 = 80 rubles, then 80 · 5 = 400 rubles. Both ways give the same answer.</p>"
  + "<h3>Inverse proportionality</h3>"
  + "<p>Sometimes it's the other way around: the bigger one quantity gets, the smaller the other becomes. A team of 2 workers paints a fence in 12 days. If there are 4 workers (twice as many), it will take 6 days (half as many). The total amount of work doesn't change: 2 · 12 = 4 · 6 = 24 &ldquo;worker-days.&rdquo;</p>"
  + "<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO_INVERSE__</div>"
  + "<p>So for inverse proportionality, the <b>product</b> stays constant: 6 workers will do the same job in 24 : 6 = 4 days.</p>"
  + "<div class=\"warn\"><span class=\"hand\">Common mistake</span>Confusing direct and inverse dependence. Ask yourself: &ldquo;If one quantity grows, does the other grow too, or shrink?&rdquo; More kilograms — higher price (direct). More workers — fewer days (inverse). Another mistake: calculating a ratio without converting the quantities to the same units (meters and centimeters).</div>"
  + "<h3>The scale of a map or plan</h3>"
  + "<p>A <b>scale</b> is the ratio of a length on a map to the corresponding length on the ground. The notation 1 : 2,000,000 means: 1 cm on the map represents 2,000,000 cm on the ground. Let's convert: 2,000,000 cm = 20,000 m = 20 km. So, 1 cm on the map is 20 km.</p>"
  + "<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO_MAP_SCALE__</div>"
  + "<p>From the map to the ground: multiply. The distance between two cities is 3.5 cm, so on the ground it's 3.5 · 20 = 70 km. From the ground to the map: divide. A distance of 90 km will take up 90 : 20 = 4.5 cm on the map. The same rule works for a floor plan of a room: at a scale of 1 : 50, a 1 cm segment on the plan is 50 cm in the room.</p>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Find the ratio of 35 to 14 and the ratio of 14 to 35.", s:"35 : 14 = 2.5 — the number 35 is 2.5 times bigger than 14. 14 : 35 = {2|5}: the number 14 makes up {2|5} of 35. Check by reducing: 14 : 35 = 2 : 5 (divide both numbers by 7).<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX0_TWOWAY__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the ratio of 2 m to 50 cm.", s:"The units are different, so convert to centimeters: 2 m = 200 cm. The ratio 200 : 50 = 4. So 2 m is 4 times bigger than 50 cm.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX1_UNITS__</div>"},
    {t:"Divide 60 rubles between Anya and Borya in the ratio 2 : 3.", s:"Total parts: 2 + 3 = 5. One part: 60 : 5 = 12 rubles. Anya gets 2 · 12 = 24 rubles, Borya gets 3 · 12 = 36 rubles. Check: 24 + 36 = 60.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX2_SPLIT60__</div>"},
    {t:"Find x in the proportions: a) 3 : 4 = x : 12; b) 5 : x = 15 : 21.", s:"a) x is a middle term: x = 3 · 12 : 4 = 36 : 4 = 9. b) x is also a middle term: 5 · 21 = x · 15, so x = 105 : 15 = 7. Check: 5 : 7 and 15 : 21 — reduce the second by 3 to get 5 : 7.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX3_FINDX__</div>"},
    {t:"3 kg of pears cost 240 rubles. How much do 5 kg of the same pears cost?", s:"The quantities are directly proportional: more pears means a higher price. Proportion 3 : 5 = 240 : x. So x = 5 · 240 : 3 = 1200 : 3 = 400 rubles. Check: the price of 1 kg is 240 : 3 = 80 rubles; 80 · 5 = 400 rubles.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX4_PEARS__</div>"},
    {t:"4 workers complete an order in 15 days. In how many days will 6 workers complete it (with the same productivity)?", s:"More workers means fewer days: this is an inverse dependence, so the product stays constant. Amount of work: 4 · 15 = 60 worker-days. Six workers will do it in 60 : 6 = 10 days.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX5_WORKERS__</div>"},
    {t:"A map has a scale of 1 : 2,000,000. The distance between two cities on the map is 3.5 cm. Find the distance on the ground. Then find how many centimeters on the map a distance of 90 km will take up.", s:"1 cm on the map is 2,000,000 cm = 20 km. 3.5 · 20 = 70 km. The other way: 90 : 20 = 4.5 cm.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX6_MAPCITIES__</div>"},
    {t:"What does the statement &ldquo;25% of all students are in 5th grade&rdquo; mean in the language of ratios?", s:"Percentages are a ratio to 100: 25% = 25 : 100 = {1|4}. So fifth-graders make up {1|4} of all the students — that is, for every fifth-grader there are three more students of other grades.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_EX7_PERCENT25__</div>"}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Find the ratio of the number 18 to the number 6 (how many times bigger is 18 than 6).", ans:"3", hint:"The ratio of a to b is the quotient a : b.", sol:"18 : 6 = 3. The number 18 is 3 times bigger than 6."},
    {q:"A class has 12 boys and 18 girls. Find the ratio of the number of boys to the number of girls as an irreducible fraction.", ans:"2/3", reduced:true, hint:"Write 12 : 18 as a fraction and reduce by the greatest common divisor.", sol:"12 : 18 = {12|18}. Reduce by 6: {2|3}."},
    {q:"Which of these ratios is equal to the ratio 3 : 5?", opts:["15 : 9","6 : 8","9 : 15","3 : 10"], ans:2, hint:"Multiply or divide both terms of the ratio by the same number.", sol:"9 : 15 — divide both numbers by 3 to get 3 : 5. The other options reduce to 5 : 3, 3 : 4, and 3 : 10."},
    {q:"Find x from the proportion 2 : 5 = x : 20.", ans:"8", hint:"x is a middle term: the product of the outer terms divided by the other middle term.", sol:"x = 2 · 20 : 5 = 40 : 5 = 8. Check: 2 : 5 = 8 : 20 (both sides equal 0.4)."},
    {q:"Find x from the proportion x : 6 = 14 : 21.", ans:"4", hint:"x is an outer term: the product of the middle terms divided by the other outer term.", sol:"x = 6 · 14 : 21 = 84 : 21 = 4. Check: 4 · 21 = 84 and 6 · 14 = 84."},
    {q:"The number 84 was divided in the ratio 3 : 4. Find the smaller part.", ans:"36", hint:"How many parts are there in total? What is one part equal to?", sol:"Parts: 3 + 4 = 7. One part is 84 : 7 = 12. The smaller part: 3 · 12 = 36 (the larger one is 48)."},
    {q:"4 kg of apples cost 320 rubles. How many rubles do 7 kg of the same apples cost?", ans:"560", hint:"Find the price of one kilogram.", sol:"Price of 1 kg: 320 : 4 = 80 rubles. For 7 kg: 80 · 7 = 560 rubles."},
    {q:"A car travels 150 km in 2 hours. How many kilometers will it travel in 5 hours at the same speed?", ans:"375", hint:"Set up the proportion 2 : 5 = 150 : x, or find the distance for 1 hour.", sol:"In 1 hour: 150 : 2 = 75 km. In 5 hours: 75 · 5 = 375 km."},
    {q:"White and blue paint are mixed in the ratio 3 : 2. 8 cans of blue paint were used. How many cans of white paint are needed?", ans:"12", hint:"How many cans correspond to one part, if 2 parts are 8 cans?", sol:"2 parts are 8 cans, so 1 part is 4 cans. White paint: 3 · 4 = 12 cans."},
    {q:"6 workers complete a job in 10 days. In how many days will 4 workers complete this job (with the same productivity)?", ans:"15", hint:"The fewer the workers, the more days it takes. Find the total amount of work in worker-days.", sol:"Amount of work: 6 · 10 = 60 worker-days. Four workers will do it in 60 : 4 = 15 days."},
    {q:"A map has a scale of 1 : 1,000,000. The distance between two villages on the map is 6 cm. How many kilometers apart are they on the ground?", ans:"60", hint:"How many kilometers are in one centimeter on the map? 1,000,000 cm = 10 km.", sol:"1 cm on the map is 1,000,000 cm = 10 km. So 6 cm is 6 · 10 = 60 km."},
    {q:"A map has a scale of 1 : 500,000. The distance between two cities on the ground is 45 km. How many centimeters will this take up on the map?", ans:"9", hint:"First work out how many kilometers correspond to 1 cm on the map.", sol:"500,000 cm = 5 km, so 1 cm on the map is 5 km. 45 : 5 = 9 cm."},
    {q:"Find x from the proportion 1.5 : x = 3 : 8.", ans:"4", hint:"The product of the outer terms equals the product of the middle terms: 1.5 · 8 = x · 3.", sol:"1.5 · 8 = 12, so x · 3 = 12 and x = 12 : 3 = 4. Check: 1.5 : 4 = 0.375 and 3 : 8 = 0.375."},
    {q:"An alloy of copper and zinc with a mass of 2 kg 400 g is made up in the ratio 5 : 3 (copper : zinc). How many grams of copper are in the alloy?", ans:"1500", hint:"Convert the mass to grams, then find how many grams correspond to one part.", sol:"2 kg 400 g = 2400 g. Parts: 5 + 3 = 8, one part is 2400 : 8 = 300 g. Copper: 5 · 300 = 1500 g."},
    {q:"The ratio of the length of a rectangle to its width is 5 : 3, and the perimeter is 64 cm. Find the area of the rectangle (in cm²).", ans:"240", hint:"The half-perimeter is the sum of the length and width, that is, 5 + 3 = 8 parts.", sol:"Half-perimeter: 64 : 2 = 32 cm. Parts: 5 + 3 = 8, one part is 32 : 8 = 4 cm. Length: 5 · 4 = 20 cm, width: 3 · 4 = 12 cm. Area: 20 · 12 = 240 cm²."},
    {q:"A hiker walked 12 km in 3 hours. How many kilometers will he walk in 2 hours 30 minutes at the same speed?", ans:"10", hint:"Find the speed, and write 2 h 30 min as 2.5 hours.", sol:"Speed: 12 : 3 = 4 km/h. Time: 2 h 30 min = 2.5 h. Distance: 4 · 2.5 = 10 km."},
    {q:"8 pumps drain the water from a pool in 12 hours. How many such pumps are needed to drain it in 8 hours?", ans:"12", hint:"More pumps means less time. Find the total amount of work in pump-hours.", sol:"Amount of work: 8 · 12 = 96 pump-hours. For 8 hours, 96 : 8 = 12 pumps are needed."},
    {q:"A plot plan — a 6 cm × 4 cm rectangle — is drawn at a scale of 1 : 500. Find the area of the plot on the ground (in m²).", ans:"600", hint:"1 cm on the plan is 500 cm = 5 m. Find the true sides of the plot.", sol:"1 cm on the plan is 500 cm = 5 m. The plot's sides: 6 · 5 = 30 m and 4 · 5 = 20 m. Area: 30 · 20 = 600 m²."},
    {q:"Three brothers divided 720 rubles in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. How many rubles did the one who got 4 parts receive?", ans:"320", hint:"How many parts are there in total, and how many rubles are in one part?", sol:"Parts: 2 + 3 + 4 = 9. One part is 720 : 9 = 80 rubles. The oldest got: 4 · 80 = 320 rubles."},
    {q:"Two numbers are in the ratio 4 : 7, and their difference is 18. Find the larger number.", ans:"42", hint:"The difference in parts: 7 − 4 = 3 parts — that's 18.", sol:"The difference 7 − 4 = 3 parts equals 18, so 1 part is 6. The larger number is 7 · 6 = 42 (the smaller is 24; 42 − 24 = 18)."},
    {q:"A troop has 15 boys and 10 girls. Find the ratio of the number of boys to the total number of children in the troop as an irreducible fraction.", ans:"3/5", reduced:true, hint:"First find the total number of children in the troop.", sol:"Total children: 15 + 10 = 25. Ratio 15 : 25 = {15|25}. Reduce by 5: {3|5}."},
    {q:"Find x from the proportion 0.4 : 1.2 = x : 9.", ans:"3", hint:"The product of the outer terms equals the product of the middle terms: 0.4 · 9 = x · 1.2.", sol:"0.4 · 9 = 3.6, so x · 1.2 = 3.6, which gives x = 3.6 : 1.2 = 3. Check: 0.4 : 1.2 = {1|3} and 3 : 9 = {1|3}."},
    {q:"A car uses 8 liters of gasoline per 100 km of travel. How many liters are needed for 350 km (at the same rate of consumption)?", ans:"28", hint:"The quantities are directly proportional: set up the proportion 100 : 350 = 8 : x.", sol:"Consumption per 1 km: 8 : 100 = 0.08 L. For 350 km: 0.08 · 350 = 28 L."},
    {q:"The perimeter of a triangle, equal to 72 cm, was divided into sides in the ratio 3 : 4 : 5. Find the largest side (in cm).", ans:"30", hint:"How many parts are there in total? What is one part of the perimeter equal to?", sol:"Parts: 3 + 4 + 5 = 12. One part is 72 : 12 = 6 cm. The largest side: 5 · 6 = 30 cm."},
    {q:"4 servings of pancakes need 300 mL of kefir. How many milliliters of kefir are needed for 10 servings?", ans:"750", hint:"Find how much kefir is used for one serving.", sol:"Per 1 serving: 300 : 4 = 75 mL. For 10 servings: 75 · 10 = 750 mL. Answer: 750."},
    {q:"3 identical printers print a batch in 12 minutes. Petya says: &ldquo;If there are 6 printers, it will take twice as long — 24 minutes.&rdquo; He's wrong. In how many minutes will 6 such printers print this batch?", ans:"6", hint:"More printers means less time. This is inverse proportionality.", sol:"Work: 3 · 12 = 36 printer-minutes. 6 printers: 36 : 6 = 6 minutes. Answer: 6."},
    {q:"The distance between two cities is 240 km, and on the map it's 8 cm. How many centimeters on the ground correspond to 1 cm on the map? (This number appears in the scale 1 : ...)", ans:"3000000", hint:"Convert 240 km to centimeters and divide by 8.", sol:"240 km = 24,000,000 cm. 24,000,000 : 8 = 3,000,000. Scale 1 : 3,000,000. Answer: 3000000."},
    {q:"Find the ratio of 45 minutes to 2 hours. Write the answer as an irreducible fraction.", ans:"3/8", hint:"First convert the hours to minutes.", sol:"2 h = 120 min. 45 : 120 = {45|120} = {3|8} (reduced by 15). Answer: {3|8}.", reduced:true},
    {q:"Kolya is now 8 years old, and his dad is 32. In how many years will the dad's age be related to Kolya's age as 3 : 1?", ans:"4", hint:"The age difference doesn't change. In the ratio 3 : 1, the difference equals 2 parts.", sol:"The difference 32 − 8 = 24 years is 3 − 1 = 2 parts, so one part is 12. Kolya will be 12, his dad 36 (36 : 12 = 3). 12 − 8 = 4 years. Answer: 4."},
    {q:"A 0.5 L carton of juice costs 60 rubles, and a 2 L carton costs 200 rubles. By how many rubles is 1 liter of juice cheaper in the big carton than in the small one?", ans:"20", hint:"Find the price of 1 liter for each carton.", sol:"Small: 60 : 0.5 = 120 rubles per liter. Big: 200 : 2 = 100 rubles per liter. 120 − 100 = 20. Answer: 20."},
    {q:"Grandma divided apples among three grandchildren in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5. The youngest, who got the fewest, got 12 fewer apples than the oldest, who got the most. How many apples did Grandma give out in total?", ans:"40", hint:"The difference between the youngest and the oldest is 5 − 2 = 3 parts.", sol:"3 parts are 12 apples, so one part is 4 apples. Total parts: 2 + 3 + 5 = 10, apples: 10 · 4 = 40 (8, 12, and 20). Answer: 40."},
    {q:"On an apartment floor plan with a scale of 1 : 200, a room is a 2.5 cm × 2 cm rectangle. Find the actual area of the room (in m²).", ans:"20", hint:"1 cm on the plan is 200 cm = 2 m. Find the actual length and width.", sol:"Length: 2.5 · 2 = 5 m, width: 2 · 2 = 4 m. Area: 5 · 4 = 20 m². Answer: 20."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"A basket has red and green apples in the ratio 5 : 3. When 8 green apples were added to the basket, the numbers of red and green apples became equal. How many apples were in the basket at first?", ans:"32", hint:"Let red be 5 parts and green 3 parts. What does &ldquo;became equal&rdquo; mean in terms of parts?", sol:"Red is 5 parts, green is 3 parts. 8 green apples were added, and they became equal to red — so 8 apples is the difference 5 − 3 = 2 parts. One part is 4 apples. At first: 5 · 4 = 20 red and 3 · 4 = 12 green, 32 total. Check: 12 + 8 = 20."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the ratio of the number 45 to the number 9.", ans:"5", sol:"45 : 9 = 5."},
    {q:"Find x from the proportion x : 9 = 20 : 15.", ans:"12", sol:"x = 9 · 20 : 15 = 180 : 15 = 12."},
    {q:"The number 90 was divided in the ratio 4 : 5. Find the larger part.", ans:"50", sol:"Parts: 4 + 5 = 9, one part is 90 : 9 = 10. The larger part: 5 · 10 = 50."},
    {q:"6 m of fabric costs 1200 rubles. How many rubles do 9 m of the same fabric cost?", ans:"1800", sol:"1 m: 1200 : 6 = 200 rubles. 9 m: 200 · 9 = 1800 rubles."},
    {q:"5 painters will finish a job in 12 days. In how many days will 3 painters finish it?", ans:"20", sol:"Amount of work: 5 · 12 = 60 painter-days. 60 : 3 = 20 days."},
    {q:"A map has a scale of 1 : 2,000,000. The distance on the map is 4.5 cm. Find the distance on the ground (in km).", ans:"90", sol:"1 cm on the map is 20 km. 4.5 · 20 = 90 km."},
    {q:"Which equality is a true proportion?", opts:["2 : 3 = 4 : 9","6 : 8 = 9 : 12","5 : 2 = 10 : 5","3 : 4 = 6 : 9"], ans:1, sol:"Check the products of the outer and middle terms. 6 · 12 = 72 and 8 · 9 = 72 — equal, so the proportion is true. In the other cases the products differ: 18 and 12; 25 and 20; 27 and 24."},
    {q:"The sides of a rectangle are in the ratio 7 : 4, and the smaller side is 28 cm. Find the larger side (in cm).", ans:"49", sol:"4 parts are 28 cm, so one part is 7 cm. The larger side: 7 · 7 = 49 cm."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"A recipe at a different scale", b:"Find a recipe (for example, pancakes, dough, or a fruit drink) for 4 servings. Write down the amount of each ingredient. Recalculate the recipe for 6 servings and for 2 servings using a proportion. Make the small portion and check that the taste comes out the same: the ratio of ingredients hasn't changed."},
    {t:"Distance on a map", b:"Take an atlas or a map on your phone and find its scale. Use a ruler to measure the straight-line distance between your city and another city. Convert the centimeters to kilometers using the scale. Compare it with the distance a map app shows, and think about why the answers are a bit different (roads aren't straight)."},
    {t:"Price at the store", b:"At the store, pick any item sold by weight (apples, candy, cheese). Look at the price per 1 kg and work out in your head how much 300 g, 1.5 kg, and 2 kg cost. Weigh the item on a scale and compare your calculation with the amount on the receipt. This is direct proportionality: the price grows by the same factor as the mass."},
    {t:"A plan of your own room", b:"Use a tape measure to find the length and width of your room and its main pieces of furniture. Choose a scale of 1 : 50 (1 cm on the plan is 50 cm in the room) and transfer everything onto graph paper. Cut out a bed and a wardrobe from paper and move them around the plan. Use the plan to find how much free space is left in the room."},
    {t:"Inverse dependence with candy", b:"Take 24 items (candies, buttons, cards) and divide them equally among 2, 3, 4, and 6 &ldquo;guests.&rdquo; Write down how many each one got, and check that as the number of guests increases, each one's share decreases, while the product &ldquo;number of guests times share&rdquo; always equals 24."}
  ],
  steps: [
    {title:"What a ratio is", body:"<p>How do you compare two numbers? You can find out by how much one is bigger than the other (by subtraction), or you can find out <b>how many times</b> bigger it is (by division). The second way is called a ratio. It's needed everywhere: in recipes, on maps, in problems about price and work.</p><h3>The ratio of two numbers</h3><p>The <b>ratio</b> of numbers a and b is the quotient a : b (b is not equal to zero). It shows two things: <b>how many times</b> a is bigger than b, and <b>what part</b> a makes up of b. For example, a class has 12 boys and 18 girls. The ratio of boys to girls: 12 : 18 = 2/3. And the ratio of girls to boys: 18 : 12 = 3/2 = 1.5 — there are 1.5 times as many girls.</p>", exIdx:0},
    {title:"Reducing ratios and different units", body:"<p>A ratio doesn't change if both numbers are multiplied or divided by the same number (other than zero): 12 : 18 = 6 : 9 = 2 : 3. So ratios can be reduced just like fractions.</p><div class=\"warn\"><span class=\"hand\">Common mistake</span>If the quantities are measured in different units, first convert them to the same ones, or the ratio will be wrong. The ratio of 2 m to 50 cm is not &ldquo;2 to 50&rdquo; — it's 200 : 50 = 4, because we first convert meters to centimeters.</div>", exIdx:1},
    {title:"Percentages are also a ratio", body:"<p>Percentages are also a ratio: 25% is the same as the ratio 25 : 100, that is, 1/4. Percentages are studied in detail in another topic — here it's enough to remember this connection.</p>", exIdx:7},
    {title:"Dividing a number in a given ratio", body:"<h3>Dividing a number in a given ratio</h3><p>Let's divide 60 rubles between two friends in the ratio 2 : 3. This means: for every 2 parts of one, there are 3 parts of the other. The total number of parts is 2 + 3 = 5. One part: 60 : 5 = 12 rubles. The first will get 2 · 12 = 24 rubles, the second — 3 · 12 = 36 rubles. Check: 24 + 36 = 60, and 24 : 36 = 2 : 3.</p>", exIdx:2},
    {title:"Proportion and its basic property", body:"<h3>Proportion and its basic property</h3><p>A <b>proportion</b> is an equality of two ratios, for example 3 : 4 = 9 : 12. Watch how this works:</p><div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO6_PROPORTION_ANIM__</div><div class=\"note\"><span class=\"hand\">Remember</span>The basic property of a proportion: <b>the product of the outer terms equals the product of the middle terms</b>. In the proportion 3 : 4 = 9 : 12 we have 3 · 12 = 36 and 4 · 9 = 36. This property also works in reverse: if the products are equal, the proportion is true.</div>"},
    {title:"How to find an unknown term of a proportion", body:"<p>Let 3 : 4 = x : 12. Here x is a middle term. It equals the product of the outer terms divided by the other middle term: x = 3 · 12 : 4 = 9. If an outer term is unknown, it equals the product of the middle terms divided by the other outer term: in the proportion x : 6 = 14 : 21 we get x = 6 · 14 : 21 = 4.</p>", exIdx:3},
    {title:"Direct proportionality", body:"<h3>Direct proportionality</h3><p>Quantities are directly proportional if, when one increases by several times, the other increases by the same number of times. Price and quantity: 3 kg of apples cost 240 rubles. How much do 5 kg cost? Let's set up a proportion: 3 : 5 = 240 : x, so x = 5 · 240 : 3 = 400 rubles. You can also do it through the price of one kilogram: 240 : 3 = 80 rubles, then 80 · 5 = 400 rubles. Both ways give the same answer.</p>", exIdx:4},
    {title:"Inverse proportionality", body:"<h3>Inverse proportionality</h3><p>Sometimes it's the other way around: the bigger one quantity gets, the smaller the other becomes. A team of 2 workers paints a fence in 12 days. If there are 4 workers (twice as many), it will take 6 days (half as many). The total amount of work doesn't change: 2 · 12 = 4 · 6 = 24 &ldquo;worker-days.&rdquo; So for inverse proportionality, the <b>product</b> stays constant.</p><div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO_INVERSE__</div>", exIdx:5},
    {title:"Direct or inverse — how not to make a mistake", body:"<div class=\"warn\"><span class=\"hand\">Common mistake</span>Confusing direct and inverse dependence. Ask yourself: &ldquo;If one quantity grows, does the other grow too, or shrink?&rdquo; More kilograms — higher price (direct, the pears example). More workers — fewer days (inverse, the workers example). Another mistake: calculating a ratio without converting the quantities to the same units (meters and centimeters).</div>", exIdx:[4,5]},
    {title:"The scale of a map or plan", body:"<h3>The scale of a map or plan</h3><p>A <b>scale</b> is the ratio of a length on a map to the corresponding length on the ground. The notation 1 : 2,000,000 means: 1 cm on the map represents 2,000,000 cm on the ground. Let's convert: 2,000,000 cm = 20,000 m = 20 km. So, 1 cm on the map is 20 km.</p><div class=\"figure\">__SVG_RATIO_MAP_SCALE__</div><p>From the map to the ground: multiply. From the ground to the map: divide. The same rule works for a floor plan of a room: at a scale of 1 : 50, a 1 cm segment on the plan is 50 cm in the room.</p>", exIdx:6}
  ]
};


/* ---------- coord ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.coord = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to find and plot points on the coordinate plane, determine the quadrant from the signs of the coordinates, calculate distances, construct symmetric points, and find the area and perimeter of a rectangle from the coordinates of its vertices.",
  explain: "<p>On a number line, the position of a point is given by one number. To indicate a place on a sheet of paper, on a map, or on a screen, one number isn't enough: you need two. That's how the coordinate plane came about — a &ldquo;grid map&rdquo; where every point has its own &ldquo;address.&rdquo;</p>"
  + "<h3>Axes and the coordinates of a point</h3>"
  + "<p>Let's draw two number lines perpendicular to each other. The horizontal one is called the <b>x-axis</b> (the axis of abscissas), the vertical one is the <b>y-axis</b> (the axis of ordinates). The point where they meet is the <b>origin</b>, denoted by the letter O, with coordinates (0; 0). The same unit of length is chosen on both axes, and negative numbers lie to the left of and below the origin — just as on a number line.</p>"
  + "<p>The coordinates of a point are written in parentheses, separated by a semicolon: A(x; y). The first number x — the <b>abscissa</b> — shows how many steps to take horizontally (right if x is positive, left if negative). The second number y — the <b>ordinate</b> — shows the steps vertically (up or down). To plot the point A(3; −2): from the origin, go 3 right, then 2 down.</p>"
  + "<div class=\"warn\"><span class=\"hand\">Common mistake</span>Mixing up the order of the coordinates. The points (3; 2) and (2; 3) are different! Always the abscissa first (horizontally), then the ordinate (vertically). Remember: &ldquo;first you enter the building along Ox, then you go up to your floor along Oy.&rdquo;</div>"
  + "<h3>Quadrants and the signs of coordinates</h3>"
  + "<p>The axes divide the plane into four parts — <b>quadrants</b>. They're numbered counterclockwise, starting from the top right. The signs of the coordinates are different in each quadrant.</p>"
  + "<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_QUADRANTS__</div>"
  + "<ul><li>Quadrant I: x &gt; 0, y &gt; 0, for example (3; 2).</li><li>Quadrant II: x &lt; 0, y &gt; 0, for example (−2; 3).</li><li>Quadrant III: x &lt; 0, y &lt; 0, for example (−4; −2).</li><li>Quadrant IV: x &gt; 0, y &lt; 0, for example (2; −3).</li></ul>"
  + "<p>Points on the axes don't belong to any quadrant. A point on the x-axis has an ordinate of 0: for example, (5; 0) or (−4; 0). A point on the y-axis has an abscissa of 0: (0; 3) or (0; −6).</p>"
  + "<h3>Distance between points on the same line</h3>"
  + "<p>If two points have the same ordinate, they lie on the same horizontal line, and the distance between them is the difference of the abscissas (subtract the smaller from the larger). For A(−3; 2) and B(4; 2) we get 4 − (−3) = 7. If the abscissas are the same, the points lie on the same vertical line, and we subtract the ordinates: for C(−1; −5) and D(−1; 3) the distance is 3 − (−5) = 8. A minus sign before the parenthesis turns into a plus — just like in the topic on negative numbers.</p>"
  + "<h3>Symmetry</h3>"
  + "<p>Two points are symmetric about an axis if the axis is a &ldquo;mirror&rdquo; between them. Watch how this works with the point A(3; 2):</p>"
  + "<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_SYMMETRY_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<div class=\"note\"><span class=\"hand\">Remember</span>About the y-axis, the sign of the abscissa changes: (x; y) → (−x; y). About the x-axis, the sign of the ordinate changes: (x; y) → (x; −y). About the origin, both signs change: (x; y) → (−x; −y). The coordinate whose sign doesn't change stays the same.</div>"
  + "<h3>A rectangle from the coordinates of its vertices</h3>"
  + "<p>If the sides of a rectangle run along the axes (that is, along the grid lines), its dimensions are easy to find from the coordinates of its vertices. Take A(−3; −2), B(2; −2), C(2; 2), D(−3; 2). Side AB is horizontal: AB = 2 − (−3) = 5. Side BC is vertical: BC = 2 − (−2) = 4. Then the perimeter P = 2 · (5 + 4) = 18, and the area S = 5 · 4 = 20.</p>"
  + "<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_RECT__</div>"
  + "<p>For a rectangle with sides along the axes, it's enough to know two opposite vertices: the length equals the difference of their abscissas, and the width equals the difference of their ordinates.</p>"
  + "<h3>The midpoint of a segment</h3>"
  + "<p>The midpoint of a segment is the same distance from both of its endpoints. Its coordinate is the <b>half-sum</b> of the coordinates of the endpoints. On a number line, the midpoint of a segment with endpoints −7 and 5 is (−7 + 5) : 2 = −1. On the plane, for a segment with endpoints (−3; 2) and (5; 2), the abscissa of the midpoint is (−3 + 5) : 2 = 1, and the ordinate stays 2: the midpoint is the point (1; 2). For a vertical segment it's the other way around: the abscissa doesn't change, and the ordinate is the half-sum of the ordinates.</p>",
  examples: [
    {t:"How do you plot the point A(3; −2)? Which quadrant is it in?", s:"From the origin, go 3 squares right (x = 3 > 0), then 2 squares down (y = −2 < 0). The signs (+; −) mean quadrant IV.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX0_POINT_A32__</div>"},
    {t:"Which quadrant is the point (−5; 4) in? Where is the point (0; −3)?", s:"For (−5; 4), the abscissa is negative and the ordinate is positive — quadrant II. For (0; −3), the abscissa is zero, so the point lies on the y-axis (3 squares below the origin), and it's not in any quadrant.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX1_QII_AND_AXIS__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the distance between A(−3; 2) and B(4; 2), and also between C(−1; −5) and D(−1; 3).", s:"A and B have the same ordinate, so we subtract the abscissas: 4 − (−3) = 7. C and D have the same abscissa, so we subtract the ordinates: 3 − (−5) = 8.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX2_DISTANCES__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the points symmetric to P(−4; 5) about the x-axis, the y-axis, and the origin.", s:"About Ox, the sign of the ordinate changes: (−4; −5). About Oy, the sign of the abscissa changes: (4; 5). About the origin, both signs change: (4; −5).<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX3_SYMMETRY_P45__</div>"},
    {t:"The sides of rectangle ABCD run along the axes. The vertices A(−2; −1) and C(4; 3) are opposite. Find the perimeter and area.", s:"Length: 4 − (−2) = 6. Width: 3 − (−1) = 4. Perimeter: 2 · (6 + 4) = 20. Area: 6 · 4 = 24.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX4_RECT_AC__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the midpoint of the segment with endpoints (−3; 2) and (5; 2). Also find the midpoint of the segment with endpoints −7 and 5 on a number line.", s:"The segment is horizontal, so the ordinate stays 2. Abscissa: (−3 + 5) : 2 = 1. The midpoint is the point (1; 2). On the number line: (−7 + 5) : 2 = −2 : 2 = −1.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX5_MIDPOINT__</div>"},
    {t:"Three vertices of a rectangle with sides along the axes: A(−3; 1), B(4; 1), C(4; −4). Find the fourth vertex D.", s:"A and B have ordinate 1, C has ordinate −4, so D also has ordinate −4. Sides AD and BC are vertical, so D's abscissa is the same as A's: −3. So D(−3; −4). Sides: AB = 4 − (−3) = 7, BC = 1 − (−4) = 5.<div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_EX6_FOURTH_VERTEX__</div>"}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"What is the ordinate of the point B(−5; 7)?", ans:"7", hint:"The ordinate is the second coordinate.", sol:"In the notation B(x; y), the second coordinate is the ordinate, that is, 7."},
    {q:"Which quadrant is the point (−4; 6) in?", opts:["quadrant I","quadrant II","quadrant III","quadrant IV"], ans:1, hint:"Determine the signs of the abscissa and the ordinate.", sol:"The abscissa −4 is negative, the ordinate 6 is positive: signs (−; +) — quadrant II."},
    {q:"A point lies on the x-axis, and its abscissa is −6. What is the ordinate of this point?", ans:"0", hint:"At what height above the origin does the x-axis lie?", sol:"All points of the x-axis lie at height 0, so the ordinate is 0: the point is (−6; 0)."},
    {q:"Give the number of the quadrant (1, 2, 3, or 4) that contains the point (−3; −8).", ans:"3", hint:"Both coordinates are negative.", sol:"Signs (−; −) — the third quadrant."},
    {q:"Find the distance between the points A(−2; 5) and B(6; 5).", ans:"8", hint:"The ordinates are the same, so subtract the abscissas.", sol:"6 − (−2) = 6 + 2 = 8."},
    {q:"Find the distance between the points C(4; −3) and D(4; 4).", ans:"7", hint:"The abscissas are the same, so subtract the ordinates: the smaller from the larger.", sol:"4 − (−3) = 4 + 3 = 7."},
    {q:"Find the abscissa of the point symmetric to A(5; −2) about the y-axis.", ans:"-5", hint:"Symmetry about Oy changes the sign of the abscissa.", sol:"The symmetric point is (−5; −2), its abscissa is −5."},
    {q:"Find the ordinate of the point symmetric to M(−3; 6) about the x-axis.", ans:"-6", hint:"Symmetry about Ox changes the sign of the ordinate.", sol:"The symmetric point is (−3; −6), its ordinate is −6."},
    {q:"Find the abscissa of the midpoint of segment AB, where A(−4; 3) and B(8; 3).", ans:"2", hint:"The half-sum of the endpoints' abscissas.", sol:"(−4 + 8) : 2 = 4 : 2 = 2. The midpoint is the point (2; 3)."},
    {q:"The vertices of a rectangle are A(1; 1), B(7; 1), C(7; 5), D(1; 5). Find its area.", ans:"24", hint:"Find the length AB and the height BC from the difference of the coordinates.", sol:"AB = 7 − 1 = 6, BC = 5 − 1 = 4. Area: 6 · 4 = 24."},
    {q:"The vertices of a rectangle are A(−2; −1), B(4; −1), C(4; 3), D(−2; 3). Find its perimeter.", ans:"20", hint:"First find the lengths of sides AB and BC.", sol:"AB = 4 − (−2) = 6, BC = 3 − (−1) = 4. Perimeter: 2 · (6 + 4) = 20."},
    {q:"Points A(−5; 2) and B(x; 2) lie on the same horizontal line, point B is to the right of A, and the distance AB is 9. Find the abscissa x of point B.", ans:"4", hint:"To the right means x is greater than −5. The distance is the difference x − (−5).", sol:"x − (−5) = 9, so x + 5 = 9 and x = 4. Check: 4 − (−5) = 9."},
    {q:"Find the sum of the coordinates of the point symmetric to P(−7; 4) about the origin.", ans:"3", hint:"About the origin, the signs of both coordinates change.", sol:"The symmetric point is (7; −4). The sum of the coordinates is 7 + (−4) = 3."},
    {q:"Find the ordinate of the midpoint of segment CD with endpoints C(−3; −7) and D(−3; 5).", ans:"-1", hint:"The abscissas are the same — the segment is vertical. Take the half-sum of the ordinates.", sol:"(−7 + 5) : 2 = −2 : 2 = −1. The midpoint is the point (−3; −1)."},
    {q:"The sides of a rectangle run along the axes, with two opposite vertices A(−4; −3) and C(3; 2). Find the area of the rectangle.", ans:"35", hint:"The side lengths are the differences of the abscissas and the ordinates.", sol:"One side is 3 − (−4) = 7, the other is 2 − (−3) = 5. Area: 7 · 5 = 35."},
    {q:"How many points with integer coordinates lie strictly inside the rectangle with vertices (−2; −1), (3; −1), (3; 2), (−2; 2)? Don't count points on the sides.", ans:"8", hint:"Inside: the abscissa is greater than −2 and less than 3, the ordinate is greater than −1 and less than 2. List the possible values of each coordinate.", sol:"The abscissa can be −1, 0, 1, 2 (4 values). The ordinate can be 0, 1 (2 values). Total: 4 · 2 = 8 points."},
    {q:"Point A(−3; 2). Point B is symmetric to A about the y-axis, point C is symmetric to B about the x-axis, point D is symmetric to C about the y-axis. ABCD is a rectangle. Find its area.", ans:"24", hint:"Write out the coordinates of B, C, D in turn.", sol:"B(3; 2), C(3; −2), D(−3; −2). AB = 3 − (−3) = 6, BC = 2 − (−2) = 4. Area: 6 · 4 = 24."},
    {q:"The sides of a square run along the axes, the square's area is 49, and its bottom-left vertex is the point (−3; −5). Find the ordinate of the top-right vertex.", ans:"2", hint:"What is the side of the square, if its area is 49?", sol:"The side is 7, since 7 · 7 = 49. The top-right vertex is (−3 + 7; −5 + 7) = (4; 2), ordinate 2."},
    {q:"Find the area of triangle MNK with vertices M(−4; 3), N(−4; −5), K(6; −5). Hint: the angle at vertex N is a right angle.", ans:"40", hint:"Find the lengths of the legs MN and NK, and recall how to find the area of a right triangle.", sol:"MN = 3 − (−5) = 8 (the vertical leg), NK = 6 − (−4) = 10 (the horizontal leg). Area: 8 · 10 : 2 = 40."},
    {q:"The sides of a rectangle run along the axes. Its bottom side connects points A(−5; −2) and B(4; −2), and the perimeter is 30. The rectangle lies above side AB. Find the ordinate of its top side.", ans:"4", hint:"Find AB, then use the half-perimeter to find the height.", sol:"AB = 4 − (−5) = 9. Half-perimeter: 30 : 2 = 15, so the height is 15 − 9 = 6. The top side is at height −2 + 6 = 4."},
    {q:"Find the sum of the coordinates of the point A(−6; 9).", ans:"3", hint:"Add the abscissa and the ordinate of the point.", sol:"−6 + 9 = 3."},
    {q:"Point B(5; −2) was first reflected about the x-axis, and then the resulting point was reflected about the y-axis. Find the abscissa of the final point.", ans:"-5", hint:"Do the reflections one at a time: first the sign of y changes, then the sign of x.", sol:"Reflection about Ox: (5; 2). Reflection about Oy: (−5; 2). The abscissa of the final point is −5."},
    {q:"The sides of a rectangle run along the axes. One side connects points A(−5; 2) and B(7; 2), and the area of the rectangle is 96. Find the ordinate of vertex C, which lies above side AB.", ans:"10", hint:"First find the length of AB, then the height of the rectangle as the area divided by AB.", sol:"AB = 7 − (−5) = 12. Height: 96 : 12 = 8. The ordinate of vertex C: 2 + 8 = 10."},
    {q:"Points C(−2; −9) and D(−2; 7) are joined by a segment. Give the number of the quadrant (1, 2, 3, or 4) that contains the midpoint of this segment.", ans:"3", hint:"First find the coordinates of the midpoint, then determine the quadrant from the signs.", sol:"The abscissas are the same, so the segment is vertical. The ordinate of the midpoint: (−9 + 7) : 2 = −1. The midpoint is the point (−2; −1). Signs (−; −) — the third quadrant."},
    {q:"Lena wanted to plot the point A(−2; 5), but mixed up the coordinates: she went 5 squares right from the origin and then 2 squares down. Give the number of the quadrant (1, 2, 3, or 4) where her point ended up.", ans:"4", hint:"Write down the coordinates of the point Lena actually plotted.", sol:"Lena plotted the point (5; −2): the abscissa is positive, the ordinate is negative — quadrant IV. Answer: 4."},
    {q:"Three vertices of rectangle ABCD: A(−3; −2), B(5; −2), C(5; 4). Find the sum of the coordinates of vertex D.", ans:"1", hint:"Make a sketch: D is on the same vertical line as A and on the same horizontal line as C.", sol:"D has the same abscissa as A and the same ordinate as C: D(−3; 4). The sum is −3 + 4 = 1. Answer: 1."},
    {q:"Find the distance between the point K(−4; 7) and the point symmetric to it about the y-axis.", ans:"8", hint:"Find the symmetric point: its abscissa's sign changes.", sol:"The symmetric point is (4; 7). The ordinates are equal, so the distance is 4 − (−4) = 8. Answer: 8."},
    {q:"On a park map, the side of one grid square is 10 m. The gazebo is at the point (−3; 2), and the fountain is at the point (5; 2). How many meters is it from the gazebo to the fountain in a straight line?", ans:"80", hint:"First find the distance in grid squares, then convert to meters.", sol:"The ordinates are equal: 5 − (−3) = 8 squares. 8 · 10 = 80 m. Answer: 80."},
    {q:"How many points with integer coordinates lie on the segment with endpoints (−3; 4) and (6; 4), counting the endpoints themselves?", ans:"10", hint:"The ordinate of every point on the segment is 4. Which integer abscissas are possible?", sol:"The abscissa can be −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 — that's 10 numbers. Answer: 10."},
    {q:"Point M(1; −2) is the midpoint of segment AB, where A(−5; −2). Find the abscissa of point B.", ans:"7", hint:"There are as many steps from A to the midpoint as from the midpoint to B.", sol:"From −5 to 1 is 6 steps to the right. Another 6 steps: 1 + 6 = 7. B(7; −2). Check: (−5 + 7) : 2 = 1. Answer: 7."},
    {q:"Find the area of the triangle with vertices A(−2; −3), B(6; −3), and C(1; 4).", ans:"28", hint:"Side AB is horizontal — take it as the base. The height is the vertical distance from C to the line AB.", sol:"AB = 6 − (−2) = 8. Height: 4 − (−3) = 7. S = {1|2} · 8 · 7 = 28. Answer: 28."},
    {q:"Which point lies in quadrant II and is the same distance from both axes?", opts:["(3; −3)","(−4; 4)","(−2; −2)","(−5; 3)"], ans:1, hint:"In quadrant II, x is negative and y is positive. The same distance from the axes means equal absolute values of the coordinates.", sol:"(−4; 4): x = −4 &lt; 0, y = 4 &gt; 0 — quadrant II, and |−4| = |4|. (3; −3) is quadrant IV, (−2; −2) is quadrant III, and (−5; 3) has different absolute values. Answer: (−4; 4)."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"A quadrilateral has vertices (−3; 0), (0; 4), (3; 0), (0; −4). Find its area.", ans:"24", hint:"The axes cut the quadrilateral into four identical right triangles with legs on the axes.", sol:"The origin is the common point of four right triangles with legs 3 and 4. The area of each is 3 · 4 : 2 = 6. Total: 4 · 6 = 24."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the abscissa of the point A(−7; 2).", ans:"-7", sol:"The abscissa is the first coordinate, that is, −7."},
    {q:"Which quadrant is the point (5; −9) in?", opts:["quadrant I","quadrant II","quadrant III","quadrant IV"], ans:3, sol:"The abscissa is positive, the ordinate is negative: signs (+; −) — quadrant IV."},
    {q:"Find the distance between the points (−6; −2) and (−6; 5).", ans:"7", sol:"The abscissas are the same, so subtract the ordinates: 5 − (−2) = 7."},
    {q:"Find the abscissa of the point symmetric to the point (−8; 3) about the y-axis.", ans:"8", sol:"Change the sign of the abscissa: (8; 3). The abscissa is 8."},
    {q:"Find the ordinate of the point symmetric to the point (4; −9) about the x-axis.", ans:"9", sol:"Change the sign of the ordinate: (4; 9). The ordinate is 9."},
    {q:"Find the area of the rectangle with vertices (−5; −2), (4; −2), (4; 1), (−5; 1).", ans:"27", sol:"Length: 4 − (−5) = 9, height: 1 − (−2) = 3. Area: 9 · 3 = 27."},
    {q:"Find the abscissa of the midpoint of the segment with endpoints (−10; 6) and (4; 6).", ans:"-3", sol:"(−10 + 4) : 2 = −6 : 2 = −3."},
    {q:"Two adjacent vertices of a square are the points A(−2; −3) and B(−2; 5). Find the perimeter of the square.", ans:"32", sol:"Side AB = 5 − (−3) = 8. Perimeter: 4 · 8 = 32."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"Battleship on graph paper", b:"Draw two axes with marks from −5 to 5 on a sheet of graph paper. Play &ldquo;battleship&rdquo; with a friend, where ships are placed at points on the plane, and a move is naming the coordinates (x; y). After each miss, name which quadrant the point was in. The game will quickly get you used to the order &ldquo;x first, then y.&rdquo;"},
    {t:"A picture made of points", b:"Come up with a picture made of 12–15 points (a house, a star, a boat) on the coordinate plane. Write down the coordinates of the points in the order you'd connect them, and give the list to a friend so they can draw the picture themselves. Compare the results. Then reflect the picture about the y-axis: how did the coordinates change?"},
    {t:"Your room as a coordinate plane", b:"Put the origin in a corner of your room (or on the floor facing the door), let the x-axis be one wall from left to right, and the y-axis be along the other wall. Pace off the coordinates of the bed, the desk, the wardrobe, and write them down. Find the distance between the wardrobe and the desk if they stand against the same wall, and find the midpoint of the segment between them."},
    {t:"Area on the plane", b:"On graph paper, mark four points so that you get a rectangle with sides along the grid lines. Write down the coordinates of the vertices, find the sides from the differences of the coordinates, and find the perimeter and area. Check it: count the squares inside and compare with the product of the sides. Then move the rectangle to a different quadrant and make sure the area hasn't changed."}
  ],
  steps: [
    {title:"Axes and the coordinates of a point", body:"<p>On a number line, the position of a point is given by one number. To indicate a place on a sheet of paper, on a map, or on a screen, one number isn't enough: you need two. That's how the coordinate plane came about — a &ldquo;grid map&rdquo; where every point has its own &ldquo;address.&rdquo;</p><h3>Axes and the coordinates of a point</h3><p>Let's draw two number lines perpendicular to each other. The horizontal one is called the <b>x-axis</b> (the axis of abscissas), the vertical one is the <b>y-axis</b> (the axis of ordinates). The point where they meet is the <b>origin</b> O(0; 0). The coordinates of a point are written in parentheses, separated by a semicolon: A(x; y). The first number x — the <b>abscissa</b> — is the steps horizontally, the second, y — the <b>ordinate</b> — is the steps vertically.</p><div class=\"warn\"><span class=\"hand\">Common mistake</span>Mixing up the order of the coordinates. The points (3; 2) and (2; 3) are different! Always the abscissa first (horizontally), then the ordinate (vertically).</div>", exIdx:0},
    {title:"Quadrants and the signs of coordinates", body:"<h3>Quadrants and the signs of coordinates</h3><p>The axes divide the plane into four parts — <b>quadrants</b> — numbered counterclockwise starting from the top right. The signs of the coordinates are different in each quadrant:</p><div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_QUADRANTS__</div><ul><li>Quadrant I: x &gt; 0, y &gt; 0.</li><li>Quadrant II: x &lt; 0, y &gt; 0.</li><li>Quadrant III: x &lt; 0, y &lt; 0.</li><li>Quadrant IV: x &gt; 0, y &lt; 0.</li></ul><p>Points on the axes don't belong to any quadrant: a point on the x-axis has an ordinate of 0, and a point on the y-axis has an abscissa of 0.</p>", exIdx:1},
    {title:"Distance between points on the same line", body:"<h3>Distance between points on the same line</h3><p>If two points have the same ordinate, they lie on the same horizontal line, and the distance between them is the difference of the abscissas (subtract the smaller from the larger). If the abscissas are the same, the points lie on the same vertical line — subtract the ordinates. A minus sign before the parenthesis turns into a plus, just like in the topic on negative numbers.</p>", exIdx:2},
    {title:"Symmetry about the axes and the origin", body:"<p>Two points are symmetric about an axis if the axis is a &ldquo;mirror&rdquo; between them. Watch how this works with the point A(3; 2):</p><div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_SYMMETRY_ANIM__</div><div class=\"note\"><span class=\"hand\">Remember</span>About the y-axis, the sign of the abscissa changes: (x; y) → (−x; y). About the x-axis, the sign of the ordinate changes: (x; y) → (x; −y). About the origin, both signs change: (x; y) → (−x; −y).</div>", exIdx:3},
    {title:"A rectangle from the coordinates of its vertices", body:"<h3>A rectangle from the coordinates of its vertices</h3><p>If the sides of a rectangle run along the axes (along the grid lines), its dimensions are easy to find from the coordinates of its vertices: the horizontal side equals the difference of the abscissas, the vertical side equals the difference of the ordinates.</p><div class=\"figure\">__SVG_COORD6_RECT__</div><p>For a rectangle with sides along the axes, it's enough to know two opposite vertices: the length equals the difference of their abscissas, and the width equals the difference of their ordinates.</p>", exIdx:4},
    {title:"The midpoint of a segment", body:"<h3>The midpoint of a segment</h3><p>The midpoint of a segment is the same distance from both of its endpoints. Its coordinate is the <b>half-sum</b> of the coordinates of the endpoints. For a horizontal segment, the ordinate doesn't change, and the abscissa of the midpoint is the half-sum of the endpoints' abscissas; for a vertical segment, it's the other way around.</p>", exIdx:5},
    {title:"Finding the missing vertex", body:"<p>If three vertices of a rectangle with sides along the axes are known, the fourth is easy to find: the vertices of a horizontal side share the same ordinate, and the vertices of a vertical side share the same abscissa. The missing vertex takes its ordinate from its vertical neighbor and its abscissa from its horizontal neighbor.</p>", exIdx:6}
  ]
};


/* ---------- alg6 ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.alg6 = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to find the values of expressions with letters, expand parentheses, combine like terms, solve equations (including ones with decimals and negative numbers), and set up an equation from a word problem.",
  explain: "<p>A letter in math is a &ldquo;box for a number.&rdquo; While we don't know the number, or want to write a rule for all numbers at once, we put a letter in its place. That's shorter and more convenient: instead of &ldquo;the perimeter of a rectangle is twice the sum of its length and width,&rdquo; we write P = 2(a + b).</p>"
  + "<h3>Expressions with letters and their values</h3>"
  + "<p>An expression with letters is called a <b>letter expression</b>. The multiplication sign between a number and a letter is not written: 5x means 5 · x. If a notebook costs n rubles, then 7 notebooks cost 7n rubles. To find the <b>value of an expression</b>, we substitute a number for the letter and calculate following the order of operations. Let a = 3 and b = −2. Then 4a + 5b = 4 · 3 + 5 · (−2) = 12 − 10 = 2. When substituting a negative number, always put it in parentheses — that way there are fewer mistakes with signs.</p>"
  + "<h3>The distributive property and expanding parentheses</h3>"
  + "<p>To multiply a number by a sum, you can multiply it by each term and add the results: <b>a(b + c) = ab + ac</b>. Watch how it works:</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_DISTRIB_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<p>It works the same way for a difference: 3(x − 4) = 3x − 12. The number outside is multiplied by EVERY term inside the parentheses, together with its sign. But what if there's a minus sign in front of the parentheses? A minus is a factor of −1, so <b>all the signs inside the parentheses flip to the opposite</b>: −(a + b) = −a − b, and −(a − b) = −a + b. For example, −(3x − 7) = −3x + 7. But −2(x − 5) = −2x + 10, because (−2) · (−5) = +10.</p>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Multiplying the number by only the first term in the parentheses: writing 3(x − 4) = 3x − 4. Correct: 3x − 12. A second mistake is forgetting to flip the sign of the second term after a minus: −(x − 7) is −x + 7, not −x − 7.</div>"
  + "<h3>Coefficient and like terms</h3>"
  + "<p>The numerical factor in a term is called its <b>coefficient</b>. In the term 7x the coefficient is 7, in −4y it's −4, and in the term x it equals 1 (x = 1 · x), while in the term −x it equals −1. <b>Like terms</b> are terms with the same letter part: 5x and 3x are like terms, but 5x and 3y are not. To combine like terms, we add their coefficients and keep the letter part: 5x + 3x = 8x, 2x − 6x = −4x.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_LIKE__</div>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Adding terms that aren't alike: 3x + 5 = 8x (not allowed — x and a plain number are different &ldquo;kinds.&rdquo;) Or writing x + x + x = x^3. Actually x + x + x = 3x, because we're counting how many x's there are in total.</div>"
  + "<h3>Equations</h3>"
  + "<p>An <b>equation</b> is an equality with an unknown number that needs to be found. A number that makes the equality true when substituted is called a <b>root</b> of the equation. An equation is like a balanced scale: if you do the same thing to both pans (add, remove, multiply by the same amount), the balance is kept. From this come two rules: <b>1)</b> any term can be moved to the other side by flipping its sign; <b>2)</b> both sides can be divided by a number that isn't zero (usually by the coefficient of x).</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_BALANCE2__</div>"
  + "<p>Let's solve 3x + 5 = 20. Move 5 to the right: 3x = 20 − 5 = 15. Divide by 3: x = 5. Check: 3 · 5 + 5 = 20, correct. If x appears on both sides, first collect the x's on the left and the numbers on the right: 5x − 7 = 2x + 11, so 5x − 2x = 11 + 7, 3x = 18, x = 6. An equation of the form a(x + b) = c can be solved two ways: divide both sides by a (4(x + 3) = 32, x + 3 = 8, x = 5) or expand the parentheses (4x + 12 = 32, 4x = 20, x = 5).</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The order is: expand the parentheses, combine like terms, collect the x's on one side and the numbers on the other, divide by the coefficient, and check your answer. The coefficient can be negative or a decimal: −3x = 12 gives x = −4, and 0.5x = 3.5 gives x = 3.5 : 0.5 = 7.</div>"
  + "<h3>Solving a word problem with an equation</h3>"
  + "<p>The approach is: <b>1)</b> use a letter for the thing you need to find (or for whatever makes it easy to express the rest); <b>2)</b> write the other quantities in terms of that letter; <b>3)</b> find two ways in the problem to get the same number and set them equal; <b>4)</b> solve the equation; <b>5)</b> go back to the problem's question and check the answer against the conditions. Example: &ldquo;One box has x pencils, the other has 6 more. There are 40 in total.&rdquo; The equation is x + (x + 6) = 40, so 2x + 6 = 40, 2x = 34, x = 17. The first box has 17, the second has 23.</p>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Find the value of the expression 3a + 2b − 5 when a = 4 and b = −1.5.", s:"Substitute the numbers, putting the negative one in parentheses: 3 · 4 + 2 · (−1.5) − 5 = 12 − 3 − 5 = 4.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX0_SUBST__</div>"},
    {t:"Expand the parentheses: 4(x − 3) and −2(5 − x).", s:"4(x − 3) = 4x − 12 (we multiplied by both terms). −2(5 − x) = (−2) · 5 + (−2) · (−x) = −10 + 2x = 2x − 10.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX1_DISTRIB2__</div>"},
    {t:"Simplify the expression −(3x − 7) + 2(x + 1).", s:"The first parentheses have a minus sign: −(3x − 7) = −3x + 7. The second: 2(x + 1) = 2x + 2. Add them: −3x + 7 + 2x + 2 = (−3x + 2x) + (7 + 2) = −x + 9.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX2_SIGNFLIP__</div>"},
    {t:"Combine like terms: 5a + 3b − 2a + 4b − b.", s:"Terms with a: 5a − 2a = 3a. Terms with b: 3b + 4b − b = 6b (the coefficient of −b is −1). Total: 3a + 6b.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX3_LIKETERMS2__</div>"},
    {t:"Solve the equation 5x − 7 = 2x + 11.", s:"Move 2x to the left and −7 to the right (flipping the signs): 5x − 2x = 11 + 7. Then 3x = 18, x = 6. Check: the left side gives 5 · 6 − 7 = 23, the right side gives 2 · 6 + 11 = 23. Correct.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX4_BALANCE_SOLVE__</div>"},
    {t:"Solve the equation 4(x + 3) = 32 two ways.", s:"Method 1: divide both sides by 4: x + 3 = 8, x = 5. Method 2: expand the parentheses: 4x + 12 = 32, 4x = 20, x = 5. The answers match. Check: 4 · (5 + 3) = 32.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX5_TWOWAYS__</div>"},
    {t:"Solve the equation 0.6x + 1.4 = −1.6.", s:"Move 1.4: 0.6x = −1.6 − 1.4 = −3. Divide by 0.6: x = −3 : 0.6 = −30 : 6 = −5. Check: 0.6 · (−5) + 1.4 = −3 + 1.4 = −1.6.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX6_BALANCE_DEC__</div>"},
    {t:"Word problem. Three packs of cookies and a box of candy cost 260 rubles together. The box of candy costs 110 rubles. How much does one pack of cookies cost?", s:"Let a pack cost x rubles. Three packs cost 3x. Together with the candy: 3x + 110 = 260. Then 3x = 150, x = 50. A pack of cookies costs 50 rubles. Check: 3 · 50 + 110 = 260.<div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_EX7_TAPE__</div>"}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 3a + 7 when a = 5.", ans:"22", hint:"Substitute 5 for a, and remember: 3a means 3 · a.", sol:"3 · 5 + 7 = 15 + 7 = 22."},
    {q:"The perimeter of a rectangle with sides a and b is found by the formula P = 2(a + b). Find P (in cm) if a = 12 cm and b = 8.5 cm.", ans:"41", hint:"First work out the sum in the parentheses.", sol:"P = 2 · (12 + 8.5) = 2 · 20.5 = 41 cm."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 5x − 2y when x = 4 and y = 3.", ans:"14", hint:"Substitute both numbers and do the multiplication before the subtraction.", sol:"5 · 4 − 2 · 3 = 20 − 6 = 14."},
    {q:"Find the coefficient in the product 4 · a · (−5) after simplifying (write it as a number).", ans:"-20", hint:"Multiply the numbers together, and leave the letter as is.", sol:"4 · a · (−5) = (4 · (−5)) · a = −20a. The coefficient is −20."},
    {q:"Which of these expressions equals 5(x + 2)?", opts:["5x + 2","5x + 10","x + 10","5x + 7"], ans:1, hint:"Multiply 5 by each term in the parentheses.", sol:"5(x + 2) = 5x + 5 · 2 = 5x + 10."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 8(m − 5) when m = 9.5.", ans:"36", hint:"You can substitute right away and calculate inside the parentheses: 9.5 − 5.", sol:"8 · (9.5 − 5) = 8 · 4.5 = 36. Check by expanding: 8m − 40 = 76 − 40 = 36."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression −(x − 7) when x = −3.", ans:"10", hint:"Substitute −3 into the parentheses; the minus in front flips the sign of the result.", sol:"−(−3 − 7) = −(−10) = 10. By expanding: −x + 7 = 3 + 7 = 10."},
    {q:"Simplify the expression 7x + 3x − 4x and find its value when x = 5.", ans:"30", hint:"Combine like terms: add the coefficients 7, 3 and −4.", sol:"7x + 3x − 4x = 6x. When x = 5: 6 · 5 = 30."},
    {q:"Which of these numbers is a root of the equation 3x − 5 = 16?", opts:["5","7","9","21"], ans:1, hint:"Substitute each number into the equation and check the equality.", sol:"For 7: 3 · 7 − 5 = 21 − 5 = 16 — correct. For 5: 10, for 9: 22, for 21: 58 — none of these work."},
    {q:"Simplify the expression 5a + 8 − 2a + 3 and find its value when a = −2.", ans:"5", hint:"Combine a with a, and numbers with numbers: you'll get 3a + 11.", sol:"5a − 2a = 3a; 8 + 3 = 11. The expression equals 3a + 11. When a = −2: 3 · (−2) + 11 = −6 + 11 = 5."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 3x + 8 = 35.", ans:"9", hint:"Move 8 to the right side, then divide by 3.", sol:"3x = 35 − 8 = 27; x = 27 : 3 = 9. Check: 3 · 9 + 8 = 35."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 7x − 12 = 30.", ans:"6", hint:"Move −12 to the right: the sign flips to &ldquo;+.&rdquo;", sol:"7x = 30 + 12 = 42; x = 42 : 7 = 6. Check: 7 · 6 − 12 = 30."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 5x − 7 = 2x + 11.", ans:"6", hint:"Collect the x terms on the left and the numbers on the right.", sol:"5x − 2x = 11 + 7; 3x = 18; x = 6. Check: 5 · 6 − 7 = 23 and 2 · 6 + 11 = 23."},
    {q:"Solve the equation −4x + 5 = 21.", ans:"-4", hint:"After moving 5, you get −4x = 16. Be careful dividing by a negative number.", sol:"−4x = 21 − 5 = 16; x = 16 : (−4) = −4. Check: −4 · (−4) + 5 = 16 + 5 = 21."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 3(2x − 5) − 4x = 9.", ans:"12", hint:"Expand the parentheses, then combine the like terms with x.", sol:"6x − 15 − 4x = 9; 2x − 15 = 9; 2x = 24; x = 12. Check: 3 · (24 − 5) − 48 = 57 − 48 = 9."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 5 − 2(x − 3) = 3.", ans:"4", hint:"There's a minus before the parentheses: −2 is multiplied by both terms, including −3.", sol:"5 − 2x + 6 = 3; 11 − 2x = 3; −2x = −8; x = 4. Check: 5 − 2 · (4 − 3) = 5 − 2 = 3."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 0.8x + 2.4 = 0.3x + 5.4.", ans:"6", hint:"Move 0.3x to the left and 2.4 to the right, then divide by the decimal.", sol:"0.8x − 0.3x = 5.4 − 2.4; 0.5x = 3; x = 3 : 0.5 = 6. Check: 0.8 · 6 + 2.4 = 7.2 and 0.3 · 6 + 5.4 = 7.2."},
    {q:"Three notebooks and a pen cost 84 rubles. The pen costs 18 rubles. How many rubles does one notebook cost?", ans:"22", hint:"Use the letter x for the price of a notebook, and set up the equation: 3x + 18 = 84.", sol:"3x + 18 = 84; 3x = 66; x = 22. A notebook costs 22 rubles. Check: 3 · 22 + 18 = 84."},
    {q:"Someone thought of a number. If you triple it and subtract 8, you get the same result as if you add 12 to it. Find the number.", ans:"10", hint:"Use x for the number. The left side is 3x − 8, the right side is x + 12.", sol:"3x − 8 = x + 12; 3x − x = 12 + 8; 2x = 20; x = 10. Check: 3 · 10 − 8 = 22 and 10 + 12 = 22."},
    {q:"Sasha is 4 years older than Masha. In 5 years, the sum of their ages will be 34. How old is Masha now?", ans:"10", hint:"Let Masha be x years old; then Sasha is x + 4. In 5 years, 5 gets added to each age.", sol:"Masha: x, Sasha: x + 4. In 5 years: (x + 5) + (x + 9) = 34; 2x + 14 = 34; 2x = 20; x = 10. Masha is 10, Sasha is 14; in 5 years, 15 + 19 = 34."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 2a − 3b when a = −1.5 and b = 4.", ans:"-15", hint:"Substitute the numbers, putting the negative one in parentheses.", sol:"2 · (−1.5) − 3 · 4 = −3 − 12 = −15."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 6(x − 2) − 3(x + 1) = 9.", ans:"8", hint:"Expand both sets of parentheses and combine the like terms with x.", sol:"6x − 12 − 3x − 3 = 9; 3x − 15 = 9; 3x = 24; x = 8. Check: 6 · 6 − 3 · 9 = 36 − 27 = 9."},
    {q:"A mother's age is 3 times her daughter's age. In 5 years, the sum of their ages will be 54. How old is the daughter now?", ans:"11", hint:"Let the daughter's age be x; then the mother's is 3x. In 5 years, 5 gets added to each age.", sol:"(x + 5) + (3x + 5) = 54; 4x + 10 = 54; 4x = 44; x = 11. The daughter is 11, the mother is 33; in 5 years, 16 + 38 = 54."},
    {q:"Solve the equation −0.5x + 7 = 2.", ans:"10", hint:"Move 7 to the right, then divide by −0.5.", sol:"−0.5x = 2 − 7 = −5; x = −5 : (−0.5) = 10. Check: −0.5 · 10 + 7 = −5 + 7 = 2."},
    {q:"Dima expanded the parentheses like this: 7 − 3(x − 2) = 7 − 3x − 6 = 1 − 3x. He made a mistake. Expand the parentheses correctly and find the value of the expression 7 − 3(x − 2) when x = 2.", ans:"7", hint:"−3 is multiplied by every term in the parentheses, including −2.", sol:"Correct: 7 − 3x + 6 = 13 − 3x. When x = 2: 13 − 6 = 7. Check: 7 − 3 · (2 − 2) = 7. Answer: 7."},
    {q:"There are 46 apples in two baskets. When 5 apples were moved from the first basket to the second, the baskets ended up with equal numbers of apples. How many apples were in the first basket at the start?", ans:"28", hint:"Let the first basket have x apples, and the second have 46 − x. Set up the equation after the move.", sol:"x − 5 = (46 − x) + 5; x − 5 = 51 − x; 2x = 56; x = 28. Check: it becomes 23 and 18 + 5 = 23. Answer: 28."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 1.2(x − 5) = 0.4x + 2.", ans:"10", hint:"Expand the parentheses, collect x on the left and numbers on the right.", sol:"1.2x − 6 = 0.4x + 2; 0.8x = 8; x = 10. Check: 1.2 · 5 = 6 and 0.4 · 10 + 2 = 6. Answer: 10."},
    {q:"For what value of a is the number 4 a root of the equation ax − 7 = 13?", ans:"5", hint:"Substitute x = 4 into the equation and find a.", sol:"a · 4 − 7 = 13; 4a = 20; a = 5. Answer: 5."},
    {q:"Simplify the expression 4(2y − 3) − 5(y − 4) to the form ky + b. What is b?", ans:"8", hint:"Expand both sets of parentheses; the second one has a −5 in front, which flips the signs.", sol:"8y − 12 − 5y + 20 = 3y + 8. So b = 8. Answer: 8."},
    {q:"A cyclist rode for 2 hours and a pedestrian walked for 4 hours, covering the same distance. The cyclist's speed is 6 km/h greater than the pedestrian's speed. Find the pedestrian's speed (in km/h).", ans:"6", hint:"Let the pedestrian's speed be x, so the cyclist's is x + 6. Set the distances equal.", sol:"4x = 2(x + 6); 4x = 2x + 12; 2x = 12; x = 6. Check: 4 · 6 = 24 and 2 · 12 = 24. Answer: 6."},
    {q:"3 kg of potatoes and 2 kg of carrots cost 270 ₽ together. A kilogram of carrots costs 15 ₽ more than a kilogram of potatoes. How many rubles does 1 kg of potatoes cost?", ans:"48", hint:"Let 1 kg of potatoes cost x ₽, so carrots cost x + 15.", sol:"3x + 2(x + 15) = 270; 5x + 30 = 270; 5x = 240; x = 48. Check: 144 + 2 · 63 = 270. Answer: 48."},
    {q:"One side of a triangle is twice as long as the second, and the third is 5 cm longer than the second. The triangle's perimeter is 45 cm. Find the length of the longest side (in cm).", ans:"20", hint:"Let the second side be x and express the others in terms of it.", sol:"x + 2x + (x + 5) = 45; 4x = 40; x = 10. The sides are 10, 20 and 15 cm. The longest is 20 cm. Answer: 20."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"If you add 5 to a number and multiply the result by 3, you get the same thing as multiplying the number by 5 and subtracting 7. Find the number.", ans:"11", hint:"Write both sides: 3(x + 5) and 5x − 7. Expand the parentheses, then collect the x's.", sol:"3(x + 5) = 5x − 7; 3x + 15 = 5x − 7; 15 + 7 = 5x − 3x; 22 = 2x; x = 11. Check: (11 + 5) · 3 = 48 and 11 · 5 − 7 = 48."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 6x − 4 when x = −3.", ans:"-22", sol:"6 · (−3) − 4 = −18 − 4 = −22."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression 4(x − 3) when x = 5.5.", ans:"10", sol:"4 · (5.5 − 3) = 4 · 2.5 = 10."},
    {q:"Simplify the expression 9y − 4y + 2y and find its value when y = −2.", ans:"-14", sol:"9y − 4y + 2y = 7y. When y = −2: 7 · (−2) = −14."},
    {q:"Find the value of the expression −3(2 − x) when x = 4.", ans:"6", sol:"Expand the parentheses: −3 · 2 + 3x = −6 + 3x. When x = 4: −6 + 12 = 6. Or directly: −3 · (2 − 4) = −3 · (−2) = 6."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 4x + 9 = 41.", ans:"8", sol:"4x = 41 − 9 = 32; x = 8."},
    {q:"Solve the equation 9x − 5 = 4x + 20.", ans:"5", sol:"9x − 4x = 20 + 5; 5x = 25; x = 5."},
    {q:"Solve the equation −1.5x + 2 = 11.", ans:"-6", sol:"−1.5x = 11 − 2 = 9; x = 9 : (−1.5) = −6. Check: −1.5 · (−6) + 2 = 9 + 2 = 11."},
    {q:"Two boxes together have 46 pencils, and the second box has 8 more pencils than the first. How many pencils are in the second box?", ans:"27", sol:"Let the first box have x, so the second has x + 8. x + (x + 8) = 46; 2x = 38; x = 19. The second box has 19 + 8 = 27."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"A number trick with a letter", b:"Ask someone to think of a number without saying it. Tell them: &ldquo;Multiply by 2, add 10, divide by 2, subtract the number you thought of.&rdquo; You'll always get 5! Check it with three different numbers, then explain the trick using a letter: (2x + 10) : 2 − x = x + 5 − x = 5. Make up your own trick and prove it using an expression."},
    {t:"A store receipt", b:"Grab a receipt or some price tags: let one item cost x rubles, and the second cost 3 times as much. Write an expression for the cost of 2 of the first item and 4 of the second. Combine like terms and check that the result matches the actual total. Then solve the reverse problem: use the receipt's total to set up an equation and find the price."},
    {t:"Scales as an equation", b:"Get a kitchen scale or make your own (a ruler balanced on a pencil). On the left pan, put a pouch with an unknown number of coins plus 5 more coins; on the right, put 17 coins. Remove the same number of coins from both sides and explain which equation rule you're using. How many coins are in the pouch? Check by pouring them out."},
    {t:"Area on grid paper", b:"On grid paper, draw a rectangle 3 squares high and 7 squares wide, and split it with a vertical line into parts 4 and 3 squares wide. Count the squares in each part and in total: 3 · 4 + 3 · 3 = 3 · 7. Repeat for a rectangle 5 squares high and (6 + 2) squares wide. This way you'll see the distributive property with your own hands."}
  ],
  steps: [
    {title:"Expressions with letters and their values", body:"<p>A letter in math is a &ldquo;box for a number.&rdquo; While we don't know the number, or want to write a rule for all numbers at once, we put a letter in its place: instead of &ldquo;the perimeter of a rectangle is twice the sum of its length and width,&rdquo; we write P = 2(a + b).</p><h3>Expressions with letters and their values</h3><p>The multiplication sign between a number and a letter is not written: 5x means 5 · x. To find the <b>value of an expression</b>, we substitute a number for the letter and calculate following the order of operations. When substituting a negative number, always put it in parentheses — that way there are fewer mistakes with signs.</p>", exIdx:0},
    {title:"The distributive property", body:"<h3>The distributive property and expanding parentheses</h3><p>To multiply a number by a sum, you can multiply it by each term and add the results: <b>a(b + c) = ab + ac</b>. Watch how it works:</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_DISTRIB_ANIM__</div><p>It works the same way for a difference: 3(x − 4) = 3x − 12. The number outside is multiplied by EVERY term inside the parentheses, together with its sign.</p>", exIdx:1},
    {title:"Minus in front of parentheses", body:"<p>But what if there's a minus sign in front of the parentheses? A minus is a factor of −1, so <b>all the signs inside the parentheses flip to the opposite</b>: −(a + b) = −a − b, and −(a − b) = −a + b.</p><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Multiplying the number by only the first term in the parentheses: writing 3(x − 4) = 3x − 4. Correct: 3x − 12. A second mistake is forgetting to flip the sign of the second term after a minus: −(x − 7) is −x + 7, not −x − 7.</div>", exIdx:2},
    {title:"Coefficient and like terms", body:"<h3>Coefficient and like terms</h3><p>The numerical factor in a term is called its <b>coefficient</b>. In the term x it equals 1 (x = 1 · x), and in the term −x it equals −1. <b>Like terms</b> are terms with the same letter part. To combine like terms, we add their coefficients and keep the letter part. Watch how it works:</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_LIKE__</div>", exIdx:3},
    {title:"Common mistakes with like terms", body:"<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Adding terms that aren't alike: 3x + 5 = 8x (not allowed — x and a plain number are different &ldquo;kinds.&rdquo;) Or writing x + x + x = x^3. Actually x + x + x = 3x, because we're counting how many x's there are in total.</div>", exIdx:3},
    {title:"Equations and the balance-scale rules", body:"<h3>Equations</h3><p>An <b>equation</b> is an equality with an unknown number that needs to be found. A number that makes the equality true when substituted is called a <b>root</b> of the equation. An equation is like a balanced scale: if you do the same thing to both pans, the balance is kept. From this come two rules: <b>1)</b> any term can be moved to the other side by flipping its sign; <b>2)</b> both sides can be divided by a number that isn't zero. Watch how it works:</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_ALG6_BALANCE2__</div>"},
    {title:"x on both sides", body:"<p>If x appears on both sides, first collect the x's on the left and the numbers on the right. Let's solve 5x − 7 = 2x + 11: move 2x to the left and −7 to the right (flipping the signs), which gives 3x = 18, so x = 6. The check confirms it: both sides equal 23.</p>", exIdx:4},
    {title:"An equation with parentheses — two ways", body:"<p>An equation of the form a(x + b) = c can be solved two ways: divide both sides by a, or expand the parentheses. For 4(x + 3) = 32: method 1 — divide by 4, giving x + 3 = 8, x = 5; method 2 — expand the parentheses, 4x + 12 = 32, 4x = 20, x = 5. The answers match.</p><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The order is: expand the parentheses, combine like terms, collect the x's on one side and the numbers on the other, divide by the coefficient, and check your answer.</div>", exIdx:5},
    {title:"Negative and decimal coefficients", body:"<p>The coefficient can be negative or a decimal: −3x = 12 gives x = −4, and 0.5x = 3.5 gives x = 3.5 : 0.5 = 7. Let's solve 0.6x + 1.4 = −1.6: move 1.4 to the right, 0.6x = −1.6 − 1.4 = −3, divide by 0.6, and we get x = −3 : 0.6 = −5.</p>", exIdx:6},
    {title:"Solving a word problem with an equation", body:"<h3>Solving a word problem with an equation</h3><p>The approach is: <b>1)</b> use a letter for the thing you need to find; <b>2)</b> write the other quantities in terms of that letter; <b>3)</b> find two ways in the problem to get the same number and set them equal; <b>4)</b> solve the equation; <b>5)</b> go back to the problem's question and check the answer against the conditions.</p><p>Example: three packs of cookies and a box of candy costing 110 rubles together cost 260 rubles. Let a pack cost x rubles, then 3x + 110 = 260, so 3x = 150, x = 50 — a pack of cookies costs 50 rubles.</p>", exIdx:7}
  ]
};


/* ---------- geo ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.geo = {
  goal: "After this topic you won't confuse perimeter and area, you'll easily find the area of the basic shapes, and you'll be able to find an unknown angle, even in multi-step problems.",
  explain: "<p><b>Perimeter</b> is the sum of the lengths of all the sides (the length of the &ldquo;fence&rdquo; around a shape, measured in cm, m…). <b>Area</b> is how many unit squares fit inside it (measured in cm², m²…).</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_RECT__</div>"
  + "<h3>Formulas</h3>"
  + "<ul><li>Rectangle: P = 2(a + b), S = a · b.</li><li>Square: P = 4a, S = a².</li><li>Parallelogram: S = a · h (the base times the height drawn to it).</li><li>Triangle: S = {1|2} · a · h.</li><li>Trapezoid: S = {a + b|2} · h (the half-sum of the bases times the height).</li></ul>"
  + "<p>Look at the diagram: for each shape, the base a is highlighted in blue, and the height h (for the rectangle, the side b) is a red dashed line; the trapezoid also has a second base b. You can see that the height is always drawn at a right angle to the base, and that the triangle's formula has an extra factor of {1|2}.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO_AREAS__</div>"
  + "<h3>Units</h3>"
  + "<p>1 m = 100 cm, but 1 m² = 100 · 100 = 10,000 cm². 1 ha = 10,000 m².</p>"
  + "<h3>Angles</h3>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_TRIANGLE__</div>"
  + "<ul><li>A right angle is 90°, a straight angle is 180°.</li><li>The sum of the angles in any triangle is 180°.</li><li>Supplementary angles (together they form a straight angle) add up to 180°.</li><li>Vertical angles (formed when two lines cross, sitting &ldquo;across&rdquo; from each other) are always equal.</li></ul>"
  + "<p>Watch how this works: the three angles of a triangle, brought together at one point on a line, form a straight angle — 180°.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO6_TRISUM_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<h3>Inverse problems and composite shapes</h3>"
  + "<p>If the area or the perimeter is known and you need to find a side, just do the inverse operation: not &ldquo;multiply the side,&rdquo; but &ldquo;divide the area.&rdquo; It's convenient to mentally split a composite shape (for example, an irregularly shaped room) into rectangles and triangles, work out the area of each part separately, and then add or subtract.</p>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The height is a perpendicular to the base, not a slanted side. In an area problem, it's specifically the height you need. If you know the perimeter and one relationship between the sides (for example, a + b), you can plug that sum straight into the formula.</div>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Finding the area of a triangle without the {1|2}: S = a · h is the area of a parallelogram. A triangle is exactly half of that. Another mistake is confusing supplementary angles (sum 180°) with vertical angles (they're equal).</div>",
  examples: [
    {t:"A rectangle with sides 8 and 5. Find P and S.", s:"P = 2(8 + 5) = 26. S = 8 · 5 = 40.<div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO6_EX0_RECT85__</div>"},
    {t:"A triangle's base is 10, the height drawn to it is 6. Find the area.", s:"S = {1|2} · 10 · 6 = 30.<div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO6_EX1_TRIANGLE106__</div>"},
    {t:"A triangle has two angles of 47° and 68°. Find the third one.", s:"The sum of the angles is 180°. The third angle: 180° − (47° + 68°) = 180° − 115° = 65°.<div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO6_EX2_ANGLES4768__</div>"},
    {t:"A rectangle's perimeter is 32 cm and its area is 60 cm². Find the sides.", s:"Half the perimeter is a + b = 16. We need two numbers with a sum of 16 and a product of 60: these are 10 and 6 (10 + 6 = 16, 10 · 6 = 60). The sides are 10 cm and 6 cm.<div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO6_EX3_RECTINVERSE__</div>"}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Find the perimeter of a rectangle with sides 9 and 4.", ans:"26", hint:"P = 2(a + b).", sol:"2(9 + 4) = 26."},
    {q:"Find the area of a square with side 7.", ans:"49", hint:"S = a · a.", sol:"7 · 7 = 49."},
    {q:"The perimeter of a square is 36. Find its side.", ans:"9", hint:"A square has 4 equal sides.", sol:"36 : 4 = 9."},
    {q:"The area of a rectangle is 48, one side is 6. Find the other side.", ans:"8", hint:"S = a · b, so b = S : a.", sol:"48 : 6 = 8."},
    {q:"Find the area of a triangle with base 12 and height 5.", ans:"30", hint:"Don't forget the {1|2}.", sol:"{1|2} · 12 · 5 = 30."},
    {q:"Find the area of a parallelogram with base 9 and height 4.", ans:"36", hint:"S = a · h.", sol:"9 · 4 = 36."},
    {q:"A trapezoid's bases are 6 and 10, height 4. Find the area.", ans:"32", hint:"First find the half-sum of the bases.", sol:"{6 + 10|2} · 4 = 8 · 4 = 32."},
    {q:"Find the angle supplementary to a 65° angle (in degrees).", ans:"115", hint:"Supplementary angles add up to 180°.", sol:"180° − 65° = 115°."},
    {q:"Two angles of a triangle are 35° and 85°. Find the third one (in degrees).", ans:"60", hint:"The sum of a triangle's angles is 180°.", sol:"180° − 120° = 60°."},
    {q:"A room's floor, 5 m × 4 m, needs to be painted. One can of paint covers 10 m². How many cans are needed?", ans:"2", hint:"Find the area of the floor.", sol:"S = 5 · 4 = 20 m². 20 : 10 = 2 cans."},
    {q:"A rectangle's perimeter is 32 cm, its area is 60 cm². Find its longer side (in cm).", ans:"10", hint:"Half the perimeter is a + b = 16. Find two numbers with a sum of 16 and a product of 60.", sol:"a + b = 16, a · b = 60. The numbers 10 and 6 work (10 + 6 = 16, 10 · 6 = 60). The longer side is 10 cm."},
    {q:"When two lines cross, one of the angles is 37°. Find the angle vertical to it (in degrees).", ans:"37", hint:"Vertical angles are always equal.", sol:"The vertical angle equals the given one: 37°."},
    {q:"In a right triangle, one of the acute angles is 35°. Find the other acute angle (in degrees).", ans:"55", hint:"In a right triangle, one angle is 90°.", sol:"180° − 90° − 35° = 55°."},
    {q:"A square plot of land has an area of 144 m². How many rubles will it cost to fence it, if 1 meter of fence costs 250 ₽?", ans:"12000", hint:"First find the side of the square (the square root of the area), then the perimeter.", sol:"Side: √144 = 12 m. Perimeter: 4 · 12 = 48 m. Cost: 48 · 250 = 12000 ₽."},
    {q:"The area of a trapezoid is 45 cm², the bases are 4 cm and 8 cm. Find the height (in cm).", ans:"7.5", hint:"From the formula S = (a+b)/2 · h, express h.", sol:"h = 2S : (a + b) = 2 · 45 : 12 = 90 : 12 = 7.5."},
    {q:"The area of a triangle is 54 cm², the base is 12 cm. Find the height drawn to this base (in cm).", ans:"9", hint:"From the formula S = {1|2}·a·h, express h.", sol:"h = 2S : a = 2 · 54 : 12 = 108 : 12 = 9."},
    {q:"A rectangular room, 6 m × 4 m, needs to be tiled with square tiles with a side of 40 cm (0.4 m). How many tiles are needed?", ans:"150", hint:"Convert everything to the same units and divide the room's area by the area of one tile.", sol:"Area of the room: 6 · 4 = 24 m². Area of a tile: 0.4 · 0.4 = 0.16 m². Tiles: 24 : 0.16 = 150."},
    {q:"From a rectangular plot 10 m × 8 m, a rectangular corner piece measuring 3 m × 4 m was cut off. Find the area of the remaining shape (in m²).", ans:"68", hint:"Find the area of the whole rectangle and subtract the area of the piece cut off.", sol:"Area of the whole plot: 10 · 8 = 80 m². Area of the piece cut off: 3 · 4 = 12 m². Remainder: 80 − 12 = 68 m²."},
    {q:"The angles of a triangle are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. Find the largest angle (in degrees).", ans:"80", hint:"Divide 180° by the sum of the parts (2+3+4) to find how many degrees are in one part.", sol:"Total parts: 2 + 3 + 4 = 9. One part: 180° : 9 = 20°. Largest angle: 4 · 20° = 80°."},
    {q:"Two angles of a triangle differ by 20°, and their sum equals the third angle of this triangle. Find the largest of the three angles (in degrees).", ans:"90", hint:"Let the smaller angle be x, then the larger one is x + 20, and the third equals their sum. Write an equation using the fact that all the angles sum to 180°.", sol:"Let the angles be x and x + 20; the third angle = x + (x + 20) = 2x + 20. The sum of all three: x + (x + 20) + (2x + 20) = 180°, that is, 4x + 40 = 180°, 4x = 140°, x = 35°. The triangle's angles are 35°, 55°, 90°. The largest is 90°."},
    {q:"The perimeter of a rectangle is 40 cm, one side is 13 cm. Find the area of the rectangle (in cm²).", ans:"91", hint:"First find half the perimeter, then the other side.", sol:"Half the perimeter: 40 : 2 = 20 cm. The other side: 20 − 13 = 7 cm. Area: 13 · 7 = 91 cm²."},
    {q:"The area of a trapezoid is 56 cm², the height is 8 cm, one of the bases is 5 cm. Find the second base (in cm).", ans:"9", hint:"From the formula S = (a+b)/2 · h, express the sum of the bases.", sol:"Sum of the bases: 2 · 56 : 8 = 112 : 8 = 14 cm. The second base: 14 − 5 = 9 cm."},
    {q:"A room is shaped like the letter L: a large rectangle 6 m · 5 m with an extension 3 m · 2 m. Find the area of the whole room (in m²).", ans:"36", hint:"Split the shape into two rectangles and add their areas.", sol:"Large part: 6 · 5 = 30 m². Extension: 3 · 2 = 6 m². Total: 30 + 6 = 36 m²."},
    {q:"When two lines cross, one of the angles is 4 times larger than the one supplementary to it. Find the larger of these two angles (in °).", ans:"144", hint:"The sum of supplementary angles is 180°. Divide it into 1 + 4 parts.", sol:"180° : 5 = 36°. The larger angle: 4 · 36° = 144°."},
    {q:"The area of a garden plot is 0.3 ha. How many square meters is that?", ans:"3000", hint:"1 ha = 10,000 m².", sol:"0.3 · 10,000 = 3000 m². Answer: 3000."},
    {q:"Katya found the area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 7 cm like this: 10 · 7 = 70 cm². She made a mistake. Find the correct area (in cm²).", ans:"35", hint:"Which factor did Katya forget in the triangle area formula?", sol:"S = {1|2} · 10 · 7 = 35 cm². Katya found the area of a parallelogram. Answer: 35."},
    {q:"A room measures 5 m × 4 m, with a ceiling height of 2.5 m. The window and door together take up 5 m². How many square meters of wallpaper are needed to cover the walls (with no extra allowance)?", ans:"40", hint:"The walls can be &ldquo;unrolled&rdquo; into one rectangle: the length is the perimeter of the floor, the width is the height of the room.", sol:"Perimeter of the floor: 2 · (5 + 4) = 18 m. Area of the walls: 18 · 2.5 = 45 m². Without the window and door: 45 − 5 = 40 m². Answer: 40."},
    {q:"When two lines cross, four angles are formed. The sum of two of them is 110°. Find the larger of the four angles (in degrees).", ans:"125", hint:"Could these two angles be supplementary? Supplementary angles add up to 180°.", sol:"The sum isn't 180°, so the angles are vertical and equal: 110° : 2 = 55°. The angle supplementary to it: 180° − 55° = 125°. Answer: 125."},
    {q:"In triangle ABC, angle A is 2 times larger than angle B, and angle C is 60°. Find angle A (in degrees).", ans:"80", hint:"Angles A and B together account for 180° − 60°.", sol:"∠A + ∠B = 120°. That's 2 + 1 = 3 parts, one part is 40°. ∠B = 40°, ∠A = 80°. Answer: 80."},
    {q:"A 15 cm × 10 cm photo is set in a frame 2 cm wide that surrounds it on all sides. Find the area of the frame itself (in cm²).", ans:"116", hint:"Find the dimensions of the photo together with the frame, and subtract the area of the photo.", sol:"With the frame: 15 + 2 + 2 = 19 cm and 10 + 2 + 2 = 14 cm, area 19 · 14 = 266 cm². Photo: 15 · 10 = 150 cm². Frame: 266 − 150 = 116 cm². Answer: 116."},
    {q:"A rectangle with sides 11 cm and 5 cm and a square have equal perimeters. By how many square centimeters is the area of the square greater than the area of the rectangle?", ans:"9", hint:"Find the perimeter of the rectangle, then the side of the square.", sol:"P = 2 · (11 + 5) = 32 cm. The square's side: 32 : 4 = 8 cm, area 64 cm². The rectangle's area: 55 cm². 64 − 55 = 9. Answer: 9."},
    {q:"The area of a parallelogram is 72 cm², its sides are 12 cm and 9 cm. Find the height drawn to the 9 cm side (in cm).", ans:"8", hint:"S = a · h, where a is the side the height is drawn to.", sol:"h = 72 : 9 = 8 cm. Answer: 8."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"The perimeter of a square is 16 cm. It was cut into two equal rectangles. Find the perimeter of each one (in cm).", ans:"12", hint:"The square's side is 4 cm. Draw how it was cut: what are the sides of the rectangle?", sol:"Rectangle 4 × 2: P = 2 · (4 + 2) = 12 cm."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the perimeter of a rectangle with sides 12 and 7.", ans:"38", sol:"2(12 + 7) = 38."},
    {q:"The perimeter of a square is 20. Find its area.", ans:"25", sol:"Side 20 : 4 = 5, area 5 · 5 = 25."},
    {q:"The area of a triangle with base 14 and height 9.", ans:"63", sol:"{1|2} · 14 · 9 = 63."},
    {q:"One of two supplementary angles is 128°. Find the other one (in degrees).", ans:"52", sol:"180° − 128° = 52°."},
    {q:"Compare a 2 × 8 rectangle and a square with side 4. Which statement is true?", opts:["Both the areas and the perimeters are equal","The areas are equal, and the perimeter is larger for the rectangle","The area is larger for the square","The area is larger for the rectangle"], ans:1, sol:"Areas: 2 · 8 = 16 and 4 · 4 = 16. Perimeters: 20 and 16. So the second option is correct."},
    {q:"Two angles of a triangle are each 50°. Find the third one (in degrees).", ans:"80", sol:"180° − 100° = 80°."},
    {q:"A trapezoid's bases are 5 cm and 11 cm, height 6 cm. Find the area (in cm²).", ans:"48", sol:"{5 + 11|2} · 6 = 8 · 6 = 48."},
    {q:"The angles of a triangle are in the ratio 1 : 2 : 3. Find the largest angle (in degrees).", ans:"90", sol:"Total parts: 6, one part 180° : 6 = 30°. Largest angle: 3 · 30° = 90°."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"Three angles in one line", b:"Draw any triangle on paper and cut it out. Tear off the three corners and put their vertices together — they'll form a straight angle, that is, 180°. Repeat with a very acute triangle and with an obtuse one. You always get a straight angle!"},
    {t:"Your room", b:"Measure the length and width of your room (or a table) with a tape measure. Find the perimeter and the area. How many meters of baseboard are needed? How many square meters of floor? Compare it with what you see in reality, and double-check your measurements."},
    {t:"Grid squares are area", b:"A square in a notebook grid is 0.5 cm × 0.5 cm, that is, 0.25 cm². Draw a triangle on the grid, count the squares inside it, and compare the result with the formula S = {1|2}·a·h."},
    {t:"Vertical and supplementary angles with a protractor", b:"Draw two intersecting straight lines (a cross). Measure all four resulting angles with a protractor. Check that the angles opposite each other (vertical) are equal, and the adjacent ones (supplementary) add up to 180°."}
  ],
  steps: [
    {
      title: "Perimeter and area",
      body: "<p><b>Perimeter</b> is the sum of the lengths of all the sides (the length of the &ldquo;fence&rdquo; around a shape, measured in cm, m…). <b>Area</b> is how many unit squares fit inside it (measured in cm², m²…).</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_RECT__</div>",
      exIdx: 0
    },
    {
      title: "Area formulas for the basic shapes",
      body: "<h3>Formulas</h3><ul><li>Rectangle: P = 2(a + b), S = a · b.</li><li>Square: P = 4a, S = a².</li><li>Parallelogram: S = a · h (the base times the height drawn to it).</li><li>Triangle: S = {1|2} · a · h.</li><li>Trapezoid: S = {a + b|2} · h (the half-sum of the bases times the height).</li></ul><p>Look at the diagram: for each shape, the base a is highlighted in blue, and the height h (for the rectangle, the side b) is a red dashed line; the trapezoid also has a second base b. You can see that the height is always drawn at a right angle to the base, and that the triangle's formula has an extra factor of {1|2}.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO_AREAS__</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The area and perimeter of a rectangle",
      body: "<p>A rectangle with sides a and b: perimeter P = 2(a + b), area S = a · b. We plug in the numbers and calculate following the order of operations — the parentheses first, then the multiplication.</p>",
      exIdx: 0
    },
    {
      title: "The area of a triangle",
      body: "<p>The area of a triangle is exactly half the product of the base and the height: S = {1|2} · a · h. The height is measured from the vertex strictly at a right angle to the base (the red dashed line in the picture).</p>",
      exIdx: 1
    },
    {
      title: "Units for measuring area",
      body: "<h3>Units</h3><p>1 m = 100 cm, but 1 m² = 100 · 100 = 10,000 cm². 1 ha = 10,000 m².</p>",
      exIdx: 0
    },
    {
      title: "Angles and the angle sum of a triangle",
      body: "<div class='figure'>__SVG_TRIANGLE__</div><ul><li>A right angle is 90°, a straight angle is 180°.</li><li>The sum of the angles in any triangle is 180°.</li><li>Supplementary angles (together they form a straight angle) add up to 180°.</li><li>Vertical angles (formed when two lines cross, sitting &ldquo;across&rdquo; from each other) are always equal.</li></ul><p>Watch how this works: the three angles of a triangle, brought together at one point on a line, form a straight angle — 180°.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_GEO6_TRISUM_ANIM__</div><p>So if two angles of a triangle are known, the third one can always be found: subtract their sum from 180°.</p>",
      exIdx: 2
    },
    {
      title: "Inverse problems: finding a side from the area or perimeter",
      body: "<h3>Inverse problems and composite shapes</h3><p>If the area or the perimeter is known and you need to find a side, just do the inverse operation: not &ldquo;multiply the side,&rdquo; but &ldquo;divide the area.&rdquo; It's convenient to mentally split a composite shape (for example, an irregularly shaped room) into rectangles and triangles, work out the area of each part separately, and then add or subtract.</p><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The height is a perpendicular to the base, not a slanted side. In an area problem, it's specifically the height you need. If you know the perimeter and one relationship between the sides (for example, a + b), you can plug that sum straight into the formula.</div>",
      exIdx: 3
    },
    {
      title: "Common mistakes",
      body: "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Finding the area of a triangle without the {1|2}: S = a · h is the area of a parallelogram. A triangle is exactly half of that. Another mistake is confusing supplementary angles (sum 180°) with vertical angles (they're equal).</div>",
      exIdx: [1, 2]
    }
  ]
};


/* ---------- circ6 ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.circ6 = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to name the parts of a circle and disk, find the circumference of a circle and the area of a disk from the radius or diameter (taking π = 3.14), and understand how a ball differs from a sphere.",
  explain: "<p>A wheel, a coin, a plate, a clock face, a hoop — there are lots of round objects around us. Today we'll work out what makes up a circle, how to measure its circumference, and how to find the area of a disk.</p>"
  + "<h3>Circle and disk</h3>"
  + "<p>A <b>circle</b> is a closed curve, all of whose points are the same distance from one point, called the <b>center</b>. If you used a compass to draw a round shape, what you drew was the circle itself — a curve. A <b>disk</b>, on the other hand, is the circle together with the whole region inside it. Put simply: a circle is like a hoop, and a disk is like a pancake. A circle's length can be measured (like a piece of rope) — this is its circumference — while a disk has an area, which is how much space it covers.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_PARTS__</div>"
  + "<h3>Radius, diameter, chord, arc</h3>"
  + "<ul><li><b>Radius</b> (r) — a segment from the center to any point on the circle. All radii of the same circle are equal.</li><li><b>Chord</b> — a segment joining any two points on the circle.</li><li><b>Diameter</b> (d) — a chord that passes through the center. It's the longest chord.</li><li><b>Arc</b> — a part of the circle between two of its points.</li></ul>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The diameter is twice the radius: <b>d = 2r</b>, and <b>r = d : 2</b>. For example, if the radius is 7 cm, the diameter is 14 cm; if the diameter is 9 cm, the radius is 4.5 cm.</div>"
  + "<h3>Circumference</h3>"
  + "<p>Watch how it works: the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter is always the same number, <b>π</b> (&ldquo;pi&rdquo;), approximately <b>3.14</b> (more precisely, 3.14159…).</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_UNROLL_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<p>That means the circumference is <b>C = π · d</b>, and since d = 2r, <b>C = 2 · π · r</b>. Example with π = 3.14: let r = 5 cm. Then C = 2 · 3.14 · 5 = 6.28 · 5 = 31.4 cm. If the diameter d = 20 cm is given, then right away C = 3.14 · 20 = 62.8 cm. To find the radius from the circumference, we work backward: r = C : (2 · 3.14). For C = 18.84, we get r = 18.84 : 6.28 = 3 cm.</p>"
  + "<h3>Area of a disk</h3>"
  + "<p>A square with side r has area r · r = r². A disk of radius r has an area roughly equal to three such squares plus one small &ldquo;piece&rdquo; (0.14 of a square). So <b>S = π · r²</b>, where r² = r · r.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_AREA__</div>"
  + "<p>Example with π = 3.14: r = 10 cm, so S = 3.14 · 10 · 10 = 314 cm². If the diameter d = 8 cm is given, first find the radius: r = 4 cm, then S = 3.14 · 4 · 4 = 3.14 · 16 = 50.24 cm². Area is measured in square units (cm², m²), while circumference is measured in ordinary units (cm, m).</p>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>1) Confusing the radius and the diameter: the formulas C = 2πr and S = πr² use the radius. If you're given the diameter, divide it by 2 first. 2) Treating r² as 2 · r. But r² = r · r: when r = 5, that's 25, not 10. 3) Writing the area in cm instead of cm².</div>"
  + "<p>It's useful to know how the sizes affect each other. If you double the radius, the circumference also doubles (C = 2πr), but the area grows by 2 · 2 = 4 times (since r is multiplied by itself in the formula). Check it: r = 1 gives S = 3.14, and r = 2 gives S = 3.14 · 4 = 12.56, which is exactly 4 times as much.</p>"
  + "<h3>Ball and sphere</h3>"
  + "<p>In space, the role of the disk is played by the <b>ball</b>, and the role of the circle is played by the <b>sphere</b>. A sphere is a surface, like the shell of a soap bubble or the rubber skin of a ball. A ball is a sphere together with everything inside it, like a whole watermelon or an orange. A ball and a sphere have a center, a radius, and a diameter — defined the same way as for a circle: the radius goes from the center to the surface, the diameter goes through the center from one point on the surface to the opposite one, and again d = 2r. The circumference formulas come in handy if you &ldquo;cut a ball through its equator&rdquo;: a cross-section of a ball through its center is a disk of the same radius.</p>",
  examples: [
    {t:"The radius of a circle is 7.5 cm. Find the diameter. Then find the radius of a circle with diameter 26 cm.", s:"d = 2r = 2 · 7.5 = 15 cm. Radius from the diameter: r = d : 2 = 26 : 2 = 13 cm.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX0_RADIUSDIAMETER__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the circumference of a circle with radius 5 cm (take π = 3.14).", s:"C = 2 · π · r = 2 · 3.14 · 5 = 6.28 · 5 = 31.4 cm.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX1_CIRCLENR5__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the circumference of a circle with diameter 20 cm (take π = 3.14).", s:"When the diameter is known, it's simpler to use C = π · d = 3.14 · 20 = 62.8 cm. Check using the radius: r = 10, C = 2 · 3.14 · 10 = 62.8 cm.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX2_CIRCLEND20__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the area of a disk with radius 3 cm (take π = 3.14).", s:"S = π · r · r = 3.14 · 3 · 3 = 3.14 · 9 = 28.26 cm². Working it out: 3.14 · 9 = 28.26.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX3_AREAR3__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the area of a disk with diameter 8 cm (take π = 3.14).", s:"First the radius: r = 8 : 2 = 4 cm. Then S = 3.14 · 4 · 4 = 3.14 · 16 = 50.24 cm². A mistake would be to substitute 8 for the radius: that would give 200.96.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX4_AREAD8__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the radius of a circle whose circumference is 18.84 cm (take π = 3.14).", s:"C = 2 · 3.14 · r = 6.28 · r. So r = 18.84 : 6.28 = 3 cm. Check: 6.28 · 3 = 18.84.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX5_FINDRADIUS__</div>"},
    {t:"A bicycle wheel has a diameter of 70 cm. How many meters will the bicycle travel in 10 full turns of the wheel (take π = 3.14)?", s:"In one turn, the wheel covers the length of its circumference: C = 3.14 · 70 = 219.8 cm. In 10 turns: 219.8 · 10 = 2198 cm = 21.98 m.<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX6_BIKEWHEEL__</div>"},
    {t:"The area of a ring. Two circles with a common center have radii 5 cm and 3 cm. Find the area of the ring between them (take π = 3.14).", s:"Area of the big disk: 3.14 · 5 · 5 = 78.5 cm². Area of the small one: 3.14 · 3 · 3 = 28.26 cm². The ring is the difference: 78.5 − 28.26 = 50.24 cm².<div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_EX7_RING__</div>"}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"The radius of a circle is 7.5 cm. Find its diameter (in cm).", ans:"15", hint:"The diameter is twice the radius.", sol:"d = 2r = 2 · 7.5 = 15 cm."},
    {q:"The diameter of a circle is 26 cm. Find the radius (in cm).", ans:"13", hint:"The radius is half the diameter.", sol:"r = d : 2 = 26 : 2 = 13 cm."},
    {q:"What is the name for a segment that joins two points on a circle and passes through its center?", opts:["radius","a chord, but not a diameter","diameter","arc"], ans:2, hint:"Remember which chord is the longest.", sol:"A chord that passes through the center is called a diameter."},
    {q:"Find the circumference of a circle with radius 5 cm (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"31.4", hint:"C = 2 · π · r.", sol:"C = 2 · 3.14 · 5 = 6.28 · 5 = 31.4 cm."},
    {q:"Find the circumference of a circle with diameter 20 cm (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"62.8", hint:"When the diameter is given, C = π · d.", sol:"C = 3.14 · 20 = 62.8 cm."},
    {q:"Find the area of a disk with radius 10 cm (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"314", hint:"S = π · r · r.", sol:"S = 3.14 · 10 · 10 = 3.14 · 100 = 314 cm²."},
    {q:"Find the area of a disk with radius 3 cm (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"28.26", hint:"First work out r · r, then multiply by 3.14.", sol:"S = 3.14 · 3 · 3 = 3.14 · 9 = 28.26 cm²."},
    {q:"A watermelon, shaped like a ball, has a diameter of 24 cm. Find the radius of this ball (in cm).", ans:"12", hint:"For a ball too, the radius is half the diameter.", sol:"r = d : 2 = 24 : 2 = 12 cm."},
    {q:"Find the circumference of a circle with radius 12.5 cm (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"78.5", hint:"Either compute 2 · 3.14 = 6.28, or find the diameter 2 · 12.5 = 25.", sol:"C = 3.14 · 25 = 78.5 cm (the diameter is 2 · 12.5 = 25 cm)."},
    {q:"The circumference of a circle is 18.84 cm. Find its radius (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"3", hint:"C = 6.28 · r, so the radius is C divided by 6.28.", sol:"r = 18.84 : 6.28 = 3 cm. Check: 2 · 3.14 · 3 = 18.84."},
    {q:"Find the area of a disk with diameter 8 cm (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"50.24", hint:"The area formula uses the radius, not the diameter.", sol:"r = 8 : 2 = 4 cm. S = 3.14 · 4 · 4 = 3.14 · 16 = 50.24 cm²."},
    {q:"A round flower bed has a radius of 4 m. How many meters of fencing are needed to go around its edge? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"25.12", hint:"The fence runs along the circle: you need its circumference.", sol:"C = 2 · 3.14 · 4 = 6.28 · 4 = 25.12 m."},
    {q:"The diameter of a wheel is 60 cm. What distance (in cm) will it travel in 10 full turns? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"1884", hint:"In one turn, the wheel covers the length of its circumference.", sol:"C = 3.14 · 60 = 188.4 cm. In 10 turns: 188.4 · 10 = 1884 cm."},
    {q:"A round tabletop has a diameter of 1.2 m. Find its area (in m²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"1.1304", hint:"The radius is 0.6 m. First work out 0.6 · 0.6.", sol:"r = 1.2 : 2 = 0.6 m. r² = 0.6 · 0.6 = 0.36. S = 3.14 · 0.36 = 1.1304 m²."},
    {q:"What happens to the area of a disk if the radius is doubled?", opts:["it doubles","it quadruples","it increases 6 times","it stays the same"], ans:1, hint:"In the formula S = π · r · r, the radius appears twice.", sol:"If r → 2r, then (2r) · (2r) = 4 · r · r, so the area becomes 4 times as large."},
    {q:"Find the length of the longest chord of a circle with radius 6.5 cm (in cm).", ans:"13", hint:"The longest chord is the diameter.", sol:"The longest chord is the diameter: d = 2 · 6.5 = 13 cm."},
    {q:"The area of a disk is 78.5 cm². Find its diameter (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"10", hint:"Find r · r = 78.5 : 3.14, then figure out which number times itself gives that result.", sol:"r · r = 78.5 : 3.14 = 25. Since 5 · 5 = 25, r = 5 cm. The diameter is d = 2 · 5 = 10 cm. Check: 3.14 · 25 = 78.5."},
    {q:"A ring is bounded by two circles with a common center, with radii 5 cm and 3 cm. Find the area of the ring (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"50.24", hint:"Subtract the area of the small disk from the area of the big disk.", sol:"S = 3.14 · 5 · 5 − 3.14 · 3 · 3 = 78.5 − 28.26 = 50.24 cm². Or directly: 3.14 · (25 − 9) = 3.14 · 16 = 50.24."},
    {q:"A shape is made up of half a disk with radius 10 cm and its diameter (the shape's border is a semicircle plus the diameter). Find the perimeter of the shape (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"51.4", hint:"Perimeter = length of the semicircle + the diameter.", sol:"The full circle: 2 · 3.14 · 10 = 62.8 cm, half of that is 31.4 cm. Diameter: 2 · 10 = 20 cm. Perimeter: 31.4 + 20 = 51.4 cm."},
    {q:"A bicycle wheel has a diameter of 70 cm. How many full turns will it make over a distance of 21.98 m? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"10", hint:"Convert the meters to centimeters, find the circumference, and divide the distance by it.", sol:"21.98 m = 2198 cm. C = 3.14 · 70 = 219.8 cm. 2198 : 219.8 = 10 turns."},
    {q:"A hula hoop has a diameter of 90 cm. How many meters would be needed to wrap around it once? Take π = 3.14 (answer in meters).", ans:"2.826", hint:"First find the circumference in centimeters, then convert to meters.", sol:"C = 3.14 · 90 = 282.6 cm = 2.826 m."},
    {q:"The area of a disk is 200.96 cm². Find its radius (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"8", hint:"Find r · r = S : π, then figure out which number times itself gives that result.", sol:"r · r = 200.96 : 3.14 = 64. Since 8 · 8 = 64, r = 8 cm."},
    {q:"A round pool with radius 6 m is surrounded by a path 2 m wide (the total radius including the path is 8 m). Find the area of the path (in m²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"87.92", hint:"Subtract the area of the pool from the area of the big disk.", sol:"Area of the big disk: 3.14 · 8 · 8 = 200.96 m². Area of the pool: 3.14 · 6 · 6 = 113.04 m². The path: 200.96 − 113.04 = 87.92 m²."},
    {q:"A round table has a radius of 65 cm. Its edge is to be trimmed with a ribbon 4 meters (400 cm) long. How many centimeters of ribbon will be missing? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"8.2", hint:"Find the circumference of the table and compare it with the length of the ribbon.", sol:"C = 2 · 3.14 · 65 = 408.2 cm. The ribbon is 400 cm, so 408.2 − 400 = 8.2 cm will be missing."},
    {q:"A clock's minute hand is 12 cm long. What distance (in cm) does its tip travel in 30 minutes? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"37.68", hint:"In an hour, the tip of the hand travels a full circle. What about in half an hour?", sol:"C = 2 · 3.14 · 12 = 75.36 cm in an hour. In 30 minutes — half of that: 75.36 : 2 = 37.68 cm. Answer: 37.68."},
    {q:"Olya found the area of a disk with diameter 10 cm like this: 3.14 · 10 · 10 = 314 cm². She made a mistake. Find the correct area (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"78.5", hint:"The radius, not the diameter, goes into the area formula.", sol:"r = 10 : 2 = 5 cm. S = 3.14 · 5 · 5 = 78.5 cm². Answer: 78.5."},
    {q:"Which has a greater area: one pizza with diameter 40 cm, or two pizzas with diameter 20 cm together? Find by how many square centimeters the area of the big pizza exceeds the area of the two small ones. Take π = 3.14.", ans:"628", hint:"Find the radii: 20 cm and 10 cm. Then calculate the areas.", sol:"Big one: 3.14 · 20 · 20 = 1256 cm². Small one: 3.14 · 10 · 10 = 314 cm², two of them — 628 cm². 1256 − 628 = 628. Answer: 628."},
    {q:"A cart's wheel traveled 125.6 m in 100 turns. Find the diameter of the wheel (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"40", hint:"First find how far the wheel travels in one turn.", sol:"One turn: 125.6 : 100 = 1.256 m = 125.6 cm. d = C : π = 125.6 : 3.14 = 40 cm. Answer: 40."},
    {q:"A slice cut from a round cake with radius 10 cm is exactly a quarter of the cake. Find the area of the top surface of this slice (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"78.5", hint:"Find the area of the whole disk and take a quarter of it.", sol:"S = 3.14 · 10 · 10 = 314 cm². A quarter: 314 : 4 = 78.5 cm². Answer: 78.5."},
    {q:"A disk is inscribed in a square with side 8 cm (touching all its sides). Find the area of the part of the square lying outside the disk (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"13.76", hint:"The disk's diameter equals the square's side. Subtract the disk's area from the square's area.", sol:"Square: 8 · 8 = 64 cm². Radius 4 cm, disk: 3.14 · 4 · 4 = 50.24 cm². 64 − 50.24 = 13.76 cm². Answer: 13.76."},
    {q:"The radius of Earth is approximately 6400 km. Find the length of the equator (in km), treating it as a circle. Take π = 3.14.", ans:"40192", hint:"C = 2 · π · r.", sol:"C = 2 · 3.14 · 6400 = 6.28 · 6400 = 40,192 km. Answer: 40192."},
    {q:"The radius of the first circle is 3 cm, and the diameter of the second circle is 3 cm. Which statement is true?", opts:["their circumferences are equal","the first is twice as long as the second","the second is twice as long as the first","the first is 4 times as long as the second"], ans:1, hint:"Find the diameter of the first circle and compare the diameters.", sol:"The diameter of the first circle is 6 cm, and of the second is 3 cm. Since C = π · d, the first is 6 : 3 = 2 times longer."}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"A metal hoop has a circumference of 31.4 m. It was replaced with a hoop whose circumference is 6.28 m greater. By how many meters is the radius of the new hoop greater than the radius of the old one? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"1", hint:"You don't need to find both radii: C = 2 · 3.14 · r, so a change in the circumference changes the radius by 6.28 times less.", sol:"Radius of the old one: 31.4 : 6.28 = 5 m. Circumference of the new one: 31.4 + 6.28 = 37.68 m, radius of the new one: 37.68 : 6.28 = 6 m. Difference: 6 − 5 = 1 m. (Shorter way: 6.28 : 6.28 = 1.)"},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the circumference of a circle with radius 7 cm (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"43.96", sol:"C = 2 · 3.14 · 7 = 6.28 · 7 = 43.96 cm."},
    {q:"Find the area of a disk with radius 6 cm (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"113.04", sol:"S = 3.14 · 6 · 6 = 3.14 · 36 = 113.04 cm²."},
    {q:"The diameter of a circle is 9.4 cm. Find the radius (in cm).", ans:"4.7", sol:"r = 9.4 : 2 = 4.7 cm."},
    {q:"The circumference of a circle is 62.8 cm. Find the diameter (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"20", sol:"C = π · d, so d = 62.8 : 3.14 = 20 cm."},
    {q:"Find the area of a disk with diameter 20 cm (in cm²). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"314", sol:"r = 20 : 2 = 10 cm. S = 3.14 · 10 · 10 = 314 cm²."},
    {q:"The circumference of a circle is 25.12 cm. Find the radius (in cm). Take π = 3.14.", ans:"4", sol:"r = 25.12 : (2 · 3.14) = 25.12 : 6.28 = 4 cm. Check: 6.28 · 4 = 25.12."},
    {q:"The diameter of a wheel is 50 cm. How many centimeters will the wheel travel in 4 full turns? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"628", sol:"C = 3.14 · 50 = 157 cm. In 4 turns: 157 · 4 = 628 cm."},
    {q:"By how many square centimeters is the area of a disk with radius 2 cm less than the area of a disk with radius 3 cm? Take π = 3.14.", ans:"15.7", sol:"S₂ = 3.14 · 4 = 12.56 cm²; S₃ = 3.14 · 9 = 28.26 cm². Difference: 28.26 − 12.56 = 15.7 cm²."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"Measuring the number π", b:"Take three round objects: a lid, a plate, a coin. Wrap a piece of string around the edge of each one, mark the length, straighten it out, and measure it with a ruler — that's the circumference C. Measure the diameter d (the widest point). Divide C by d. You should get a number around 3.1–3.2. Write down the three results in a table and explain why they're almost the same."},
    {t:"Drawing with a compass", b:"Use a compass to draw a circle with radius 4 cm. Draw in a diameter, a radius, and two different chords. Measure the diameter and check that it's 8 cm. Measure the chords — are they shorter than the diameter? Cut out the disk and fold it in half several times: all the folds (diameters) will cross at one point. What point is that?"},
    {t:"A wheel out for a walk", b:"Measure the diameter of your bicycle's or scooter's wheel in centimeters. Calculate its circumference using the formula C = 3.14 · d. Then roll the wheel one full turn along the floor and measure the distance covered with a tape measure. Compare the calculation with the experiment. How many turns would the wheel make on the way to school, if you know that distance?"},
    {t:"Ball and sphere", b:"Take an orange or an apple (a ball) and score the peel along its &ldquo;equator.&rdquo; The removed peel is a model of a sphere (a surface), while the inside part is the ball. Cut the orange in half with a piece of string along its diameter: look at what shape appears on the cross-section. Trace the cross-section onto paper and measure the radius of this disk."}
  ],
  steps: [
    {title:"Circle and disk", body:"<p>A wheel, a coin, a plate, a clock face, a hoop — there are lots of round objects around us. Today we'll work out what makes up a circle, how to measure its circumference, and how to find the area of a disk.</p><h3>Circle and disk</h3><p>A <b>circle</b> is a closed curve, all of whose points are the same distance from one point, called the <b>center</b>. If you used a compass to draw a round shape, what you drew was the circle itself — a curve. A <b>disk</b>, on the other hand, is the circle together with the whole region inside it. Put simply: a circle is like a hoop, and a disk is like a pancake. A circle's length can be measured (like a piece of rope) — this is its circumference — while a disk has an area, which is how much space it covers.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_PARTS__</div>"},
    {title:"Radius, diameter, chord, arc", body:"<ul><li><b>Radius</b> (r) — a segment from the center to any point on the circle. All radii of the same circle are equal.</li><li><b>Chord</b> — a segment joining any two points on the circle.</li><li><b>Diameter</b> (d) — a chord that passes through the center. It's the longest chord.</li><li><b>Arc</b> — a part of the circle between two of its points.</li></ul><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>The diameter is twice the radius: <b>d = 2r</b>, and <b>r = d : 2</b>. For example, if the radius is 7 cm, the diameter is 14 cm; if the diameter is 9 cm, the radius is 4.5 cm.</div>", exIdx:0},
    {title:"Circumference: C = 2πr", body:"<h3>Circumference</h3><p>Watch how it works: the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter is always the same number, <b>π</b> (&ldquo;pi&rdquo;), approximately <b>3.14</b> (more precisely, 3.14159…).</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_UNROLL_ANIM__</div><p>That means the circumference is <b>C = π · d</b>, and since d = 2r, <b>C = 2 · π · r</b>. Example with π = 3.14: let r = 5 cm. Then C = 2 · 3.14 · 5 = 6.28 · 5 = 31.4 cm.</p>", exIdx:1},
    {title:"Circumference from the diameter", body:"<p>If the diameter d = 20 cm is given, then right away C = π · d = 3.14 · 20 = 62.8 cm — it's simpler to calculate this way, multiplying the diameter by π directly, without multiplying by 2.</p>", exIdx:2},
    {title:"Finding the radius from the circumference", body:"<p>To find the radius from the circumference, we work backward: r = C : (2 · 3.14). For C = 18.84, we get r = 18.84 : 6.28 = 3 cm.</p><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Since C = 2 · π · r, to find r you need to divide C by 2 · π, that is, by 6.28.</div>", exIdx:5},
    {title:"Area of a disk: S = πr²", body:"<h3>Area of a disk</h3><p>A square with side r has area r · r = r². A disk of radius r has an area roughly equal to three such squares plus one small &ldquo;piece&rdquo; (0.14 of a square). So <b>S = π · r²</b>, where r² = r · r.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_AREA__</div><p>Example with π = 3.14: r = 3 cm, so S = 3.14 · 3 · 3 = 3.14 · 9 = 28.26 cm². Working it out: 3.14 · 9 = 28.26.</p>", exIdx:3},
    {title:"Area of a disk from the diameter", body:"<p>If the diameter d = 8 cm is given, first find the radius: r = 8 : 2 = 4 cm. Then S = 3.14 · 4 · 4 = 3.14 · 16 = 50.24 cm².</p><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>1) Confusing the radius and the diameter: the formulas C = 2πr and S = πr² use the radius. If you're given the diameter, divide it by 2 first. A mistake would be to substitute 8 for the radius: that would give 200.96. 2) Treating r² as 2 · r. But r² = r · r: when r = 5, that's 25, not 10. 3) Writing the area in cm instead of cm².</div>", exIdx:4},
    {title:"How the radius affects the circumference and the area", body:"<p>It's useful to know how the sizes affect each other. If you double the radius, the circumference also doubles (C = 2πr), but the area grows by 2 · 2 = 4 times (since r is multiplied by itself in the formula). Check it: r = 1 gives S = 3.14, and r = 2 gives S = 3.14 · 4 = 12.56, which is exactly 4 times as much.</p><p>The problem about the bicycle wheel with diameter 70 cm shows this same formula C = π · d in action: in one turn, the wheel covers the length of its circumference C = 3.14 · 70 = 219.8 cm, and in 10 turns — 219.8 · 10 = 2198 cm = 21.98 m.</p>", exIdx:6},
    {title:"Area of a ring", body:"<p>Sometimes you need to find the area of a ring between two circles with a common center. To do this, find the area of the big disk and the area of the small disk separately, then subtract: the area of the big disk is 3.14 · 5 · 5 = 78.5 cm², and the area of the small one is 3.14 · 3 · 3 = 28.26 cm². The ring is the difference: 78.5 − 28.26 = 50.24 cm².</p>", exIdx:7},
    {title:"Ball and sphere", body:"<h3>Ball and sphere</h3><p>In space, the role of the disk is played by the <b>ball</b>, and the role of the circle is played by the <b>sphere</b>. A sphere is a surface, like the shell of a soap bubble or the rubber skin of a ball. A ball is a sphere together with everything inside it, like a whole watermelon or an orange. A ball and a sphere have a center, a radius, and a diameter — defined the same way as for a circle: the radius goes from the center to the surface, the diameter goes through the center from one point on the surface to the opposite one, and again d = 2r. The circumference formulas come in handy if you &ldquo;cut a ball through its equator&rdquo;: a cross-section of a ball through its center is a disk of the same radius.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_CIRC6_PARTS__</div>"}
  ]
};


/* ---------- stat6 ---------- */
CONTENT_EN.stat6 = {
  goal: "After this topic you'll be able to find the arithmetic mean, range, mode and median, read tables and charts, solve problems on average price and average speed, and find the probability of simple events as the share of favorable outcomes.",
  explain: "<p>When you need to quickly understand what's going on with a set of numbers, you don't look through every single one — you find one number that describes the whole set: &ldquo;the average grade,&rdquo; &ldquo;the average temperature,&rdquo; &ldquo;the typical price.&rdquo; That's what statistics does. Its close friend is probability: it measures how likely an event is.</p>"
  + "<h3>Arithmetic mean</h3><p>Watch how the blocks even out into equal columns — that's how you find the <b>arithmetic mean</b>.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_MEAN_ANIM__</div>"
  + "<div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Arithmetic mean = sum of all the numbers : the number of numbers. For our columns, (3 + 9 + 4 + 8) : 4 = 24 : 4 = 6. The mean always lies between the smallest and the largest number in the set.</div>"
  + "<p>Some useful things follow from the formula. If you know the mean and the number of values, then <b>sum = mean · count</b>. For example, if the mean of four numbers is 15, their sum is 15 · 4 = 60.</p>"
  + "<h3>Range, mode, median</h3><p>Take the set: 5, 2, 8, 5, 3, 9, 5. The <b>range</b> is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers: 9 − 2 = 7. It shows how &ldquo;spread out&rdquo; the numbers are. The <b>mode</b> is the number that appears most often in the set: here it's 5 (three times). The <b>median</b> is the number in the middle of the set once it's written in increasing order: 2, 3, 5, 5, 5, 8, 9 — the middle number is 5. If there's an even number of values, the median equals the arithmetic mean of the two middle numbers: for 3, 5, 8, 10 that's (5 + 8) : 2 = 6.5.</p>"
  + "<div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Looking for the median without writing the numbers in order first: taking the number that sits in the middle of the original list. Always order the set first! Another mistake is thinking the range and the mean are the same thing. The range is &ldquo;how wide,&rdquo; the mean is &ldquo;around which number.&rdquo;</div>"
  + "<h3>Tables and charts</h3><p>Data is written into a table or drawn as a picture. In a <b>bar chart</b>, the height of a bar equals its value, which makes comparing easy. In a <b>pie chart</b>, the whole circle is the total (100%, 360°), and a slice shows a share. The bigger the slice's angle, the bigger the share: 180° is a half, 90° is a quarter, 36° is 36 : 360 = 0.1, that is, 10%.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_PIE__</div>"
  + "<p>To find how many people are in a slice, find that share of the total number. If 120 people took the survey, and the &ldquo;Bananas&rdquo; slice has an angle of 90°, then the share {90|360} = {1|4}, and {1|4} of 120 is 30 people.</p>"
  + "<h3>Average price and average speed</h3><p>Say you bought 3 kg of apples at 40 rubles per kilogram and 2 kg of pears at 50 rubles. The average price isn't (40 + 50) : 2! We work out the total cost: 3 · 40 + 2 · 50 = 220 rubles for 5 kg. The average price is 220 : 5 = 44 rubles per kg. Rule: <b>average value = &ldquo;total&rdquo; : &ldquo;total count&rdquo;</b>. It works the same way with speed: <b>average speed = whole distance : whole time</b>. A car drove 2 hours at 60 km/h and 3 hours at 80 km/h. The distance is 120 + 240 = 360 km, the time is 5 h, the average speed is 360 : 5 = 72 km/h, not (60 + 80) : 2 = 70.</p>"
  + "<h3>How the mean changes</h3><p>If you add a number <i>greater</i> than the mean to the set, the mean goes up; if it's <i>smaller</i>, the mean goes down. We work it out through sums. The mean of five numbers is 12, so their sum is 60. We add 18: the sum becomes 78, there are now 6 numbers, and the new mean is 78 : 6 = 13.</p>"
  + "<h3>Probability</h3><p>In simple cases, where all outcomes are equally likely, <b>the probability of an event = the number of favorable outcomes : the total number of outcomes</b>. A coin: it lands heads or tails, there are 2 outcomes in total, 1 is favorable, the probability is {1|2}. A die: there are 6 outcomes in total, the probability of rolling an even number (2, 4, 6) is {3|6} = {1|2}. A bag with 3 red balls and 7 blue balls: the probability of drawing a red one is {3|10}. Probability is always between 0 (an impossible event) and 1 (a certain one). For two dice, look at the table: there are 36 outcomes in total.</p>"
  + "<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_DICE__</div>"
  + "<p>A sum of 7 comes up in 6 cells, so the probability is {6|36} = {1|6}. It's standard to reduce the fraction.</p>",
  examples: [
    {t:"Find the arithmetic mean of the numbers 4, 7, 9, 12, 8.", s:"Sum: 4 + 7 + 9 + 12 + 8 = 40. There are 5 numbers. Mean: 40 : 5 = 8.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX0_MEAN47912__</div>"},
    {t:"For the set 3, 8, 5, 10, find the range and the median.", s:"Range: 10 − 3 = 7. Let's order them: 3, 5, 8, 10. There are four numbers, the middle ones are 5 and 8, so the median is (5 + 8) : 2 = 6.5.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX1_RANGEMEDIAN__</div>"},
    {t:"Find the mode of the set: 3, 5, 2, 5, 7, 3, 5.", s:"Let's count how many times each number appears: 3 — twice, 5 — three times, 2 and 7 — once each. The number 5 appears most often, so that's the mode.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX2_MODE3525735__</div>"},
    {t:"A bar chart shows how many books five students read in a week: 6, 4, 9, 5, 6. By how much does the greatest value exceed the mean?", s:"Sum 6 + 4 + 9 + 5 + 6 = 30. Mean 30 : 5 = 6. The greatest value is 9, so 9 − 6 = 3.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX3_BOOKS64956__</div>"},
    {t:"2 kg of candy at 120 rubles per kg and 3 kg of cookies at 80 rubles per kg were bought. Find the average price per kilogram.", s:"Total cost: 2 · 120 + 3 · 80 = 240 + 240 = 480 rubles. In total, 5 kg. Average price: 480 : 5 = 96 rubles per kg. It would be a mistake to calculate (120 + 80) : 2 = 100.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX4_AVGPRICE__</div>"},
    {t:"A hiker walked 12 km in 3 hours, then another 8 km in 2 hours. Find his average speed.", s:"Whole distance: 12 + 8 = 20 km. Whole time: 3 + 2 = 5 h. Average speed: 20 : 5 = 4 km/h.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX5_AVGSPEED__</div>"},
    {t:"The arithmetic mean of six numbers is 8. The number 15 was added to them. What's the mean of the seven numbers?", s:"Sum of six numbers: 8 · 6 = 48. After adding: 48 + 15 = 63, with 7 numbers. Mean: 63 : 7 = 9. It went up, because 15 is greater than the old mean of 8.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX6_MEANSHIFT__</div>"},
    {t:"There are 4 white and 6 black balls in a bag. Find the probability of drawing a white ball.", s:"There are 4 + 6 = 10 outcomes in total, 4 favorable. The probability {4|10} = {2|5} after reducing by 2.<div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_EX7_BAGPROB__</div>"}
  ],
  tasks: [
    {q:"Find the arithmetic mean of the numbers 4, 7, 9 and 12.", ans:"8", hint:"Add the numbers and divide the sum by their count.", sol:"(4 + 7 + 9 + 12) : 4 = 32 : 4 = 8."},
    {q:"Anya rolls a standard die (faces numbered 1 through 6). Which event is impossible?", opts:["a 6 comes up","an even number comes up","a number greater than 6 comes up","a 1 comes up"], ans:2, hint:"An impossible event can't happen on any roll: look at which numbers are on the die.", sol:"The die has numbers from 1 to 6, so a number greater than 6 can never come up. The other events are possible."},
    {q:"Find the range of the set of numbers: 23, 8, 15, 31, 12.", ans:"23", hint:"The range is the largest number minus the smallest.", sol:"The largest is 31, the smallest is 8. Range: 31 − 8 = 23."},
    {q:"Find the mode of the set: 3, 5, 2, 5, 7, 3, 5.", ans:"5", hint:"The mode is the most frequent number.", sol:"The number 5 appears 3 times, the number 3 appears 2 times, the rest once each. The mode is 5."},
    {q:"Find the median of the set: 2, 9, 4, 7, 5.", opts:["4","5","7","9"], ans:1, hint:"First write the numbers in increasing order.", sol:"Ordered: 2, 4, 5, 7, 9. The middle number is 5."},
    {q:"Find the median of the set: 3, 8, 5, 10.", ans:"6.5", hint:"Order the numbers first. There's an even number of them — take the mean of the two middle ones.", sol:"Ordered: 3, 5, 8, 10. The middle numbers are 5 and 8: (5 + 8) : 2 = 6.5."},
    {q:"A die is rolled once. Find the probability that an even number of pips comes up (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"1/2", reduced:true, hint:"The even numbers on a die are 2, 4, 6. Probability = favorable : total.", sol:"There are 3 favorable outcomes (2, 4, 6), 6 in total. Probability 3/6 = 1/2."},
    {q:"A bag has 3 red, 5 blue and 2 green balls. Find the probability of drawing a green ball at random (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"1/5", reduced:true, hint:"First find the total number of balls.", sol:"In total there are 3 + 5 + 2 = 10 balls, 2 of them green. Probability 2/10 = 1/5."},
    {q:"The midday air temperature over a week (in °C): 12, 15, 11, 14, 18, 16, 12. Find the average temperature for the week.", ans:"14", hint:"Add up all seven numbers and divide by 7.", sol:"Sum: 12 + 15 + 11 + 14 + 18 + 16 + 12 = 98. Mean 98 : 7 = 14 °C."},
    {q:"3 kg of apples at 40 rubles per kilogram and 2 kg of pears at 50 rubles per kilogram were bought. Find the average price of one kilogram of fruit (in rubles).", ans:"44", hint:"Find the total cost of the purchase and divide by the total mass.", sol:"Cost: 3 · 40 + 2 · 50 = 120 + 100 = 220 rubles. Mass 5 kg. Average price 220 : 5 = 44 rubles."},
    {q:"A car drove for 2 hours at 60 km/h, then for 3 hours at 80 km/h. Find the average speed over the whole trip (in km/h).", ans:"72", hint:"Average speed = whole distance : whole time. Find each distance separately.", sol:"Distance: 60 · 2 + 80 · 3 = 120 + 240 = 360 km. Time 2 + 3 = 5 h. Speed 360 : 5 = 72 km/h."},
    {q:"The arithmetic mean of five numbers is 12. The number 18 was added to them. What is the arithmetic mean of all six numbers?", ans:"13", hint:"Find the sum of the five numbers: mean · count.", sol:"Sum of five numbers: 12 · 5 = 60. With the new number: 60 + 18 = 78. Mean 78 : 6 = 13."},
    {q:"The arithmetic mean of four numbers is 15. Three of them are 12, 14 and 20. Find the fourth number.", ans:"14", hint:"Find the sum of the four numbers, then subtract the three known ones.", sol:"Sum of four: 15 · 4 = 60. The three known ones give 12 + 14 + 20 = 46. Fourth: 60 − 46 = 14."},
    {q:"240 people took part in a survey. On the pie chart, the &ldquo;swimming&rdquo; slice has an angle of 105°. How many people chose swimming?", ans:"70", hint:"The whole circle — 360° — is 240 people. How many people does 15° correspond to?", sol:"360° : 15° = 24, so each 15° corresponds to 240 : 24 = 10 people. 105° = 7 · 15°, so 7 · 10 = 70 people."},
    {q:"A store sold 24, 18, 30, 12 and 26 books over five days. How many more books were sold on the best day than the daily average?", ans:"8", hint:"Find the average per day, then compare it to the largest number.", sol:"Sum: 24 + 18 + 30 + 12 + 26 = 110. Mean 110 : 5 = 22. The largest is 30. Difference 30 − 22 = 8."},
    {q:"Petya has four dictation grades: 4, 5, 3 and 4. What grade does he need on the fifth dictation for his average grade to become 4.2?", ans:"5", hint:"What should the sum of five grades be for the average to be 4.2? What is it now?", sol:"Needed sum: 4.2 · 5 = 21. Currently: 4 + 5 + 3 + 4 = 16. He needs to get 21 − 16 = 5."},
    {q:"The arithmetic mean of six numbers is 9. One of them, the number 15, was replaced by 3. By how much did the arithmetic mean decrease?", ans:"2", hint:"By how much did the sum decrease? How does that affect the mean, if there are 6 numbers?", sol:"The sum decreased by 15 − 3 = 12. The mean decreased by 12 : 6 = 2. Check: it was 54 : 6 = 9, now it's 42 : 6 = 7."},
    {q:"Two dice are rolled. Find the probability that the sum of the pips equals 5 (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"1/9", reduced:true, hint:"There are 6 · 6 = 36 outcomes in total. List the pairs with a sum of 5: (1; 4) and so on.", sol:"Pairs with a sum of 5: (1; 4), (2; 3), (3; 2), (4; 1) — 4 of them. There are 36 outcomes in total. Probability 4/36 = 1/9."},
    {q:"From town A to town B, 120 km apart, a bus traveled at 60 km/h, and came back at 40 km/h. Find the average speed over the whole trip (in km/h).", ans:"48", hint:"The average speed is not (60 + 40) : 2. Find the time in each direction.", sol:"There: 120 : 60 = 2 h. Back: 120 : 40 = 3 h. Whole distance 240 km, whole time 5 h. Speed 240 : 5 = 48 km/h."},
    {q:"The average age of 11 players on a football team is 22. During the match one player was sent off, and the average age of the remaining 10 became 21. How old is the player who was sent off?", ans:"32", hint:"Find the sum of ages of all 11 and the sum of ages of the remaining 10.", sol:"Sum of ages of 11 players: 22 · 11 = 242. Sum of the remaining 10: 21 · 10 = 210. Age of the player sent off: 242 − 210 = 32."},
    {q:"Over 5 training sessions an athlete ran (in km): 8, 10, 6, 12, 9. Find the average distance per session.", ans:"9", hint:"Add up all the numbers and divide by their count.", sol:"(8 + 10 + 6 + 12 + 9) : 5 = 45 : 5 = 9."},
    {q:"Find the median of the set: 12, 5, 19, 5, 8, 15, 5.", ans:"8", hint:"First write the numbers in increasing order.", sol:"Ordered: 5, 5, 5, 8, 12, 15, 19. There are 7 numbers, the middle (4th) number is 8."},
    {q:"A deck has 36 cards: 9 cards of each of the four suits. Find the probability that a card drawn at random is a diamond (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"1/4", reduced:true, hint:"Divide the number of favorable outcomes by the total number of cards.", sol:"There are 9 favorable outcomes, 36 in total. Probability 9/36 = 1/4."},
    {q:"The arithmetic mean of seven numbers is 10. One of them, equal to 25, was replaced by 4. Find the new arithmetic mean of these seven numbers.", ans:"7", hint:"Find the old sum of the seven numbers, then replace one term.", sol:"Old sum: 10 · 7 = 70. New sum: 70 − 25 + 4 = 49. New mean: 49 : 7 = 7."},
    {q:"On a test, a class got: &ldquo;5&rdquo; — 6 people, &ldquo;4&rdquo; — 10 people, &ldquo;3&rdquo; — 8 people, &ldquo;2&rdquo; — 1 person. Find the class's average grade.", ans:"3.84", hint:"Find the sum of all the grades and divide by the number of students.", sol:"Sum: 5 · 6 + 4 · 10 + 3 · 8 + 2 · 1 = 30 + 40 + 24 + 2 = 96. Students: 6 + 10 + 8 + 1 = 25. 96 : 25 = 3.84. Answer: 3.84."},
    {q:"There are 25 students in a class, 10 of them girls. A classroom monitor is chosen at random. Find the probability that the monitor turns out to be a boy (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"3/5", hint:"First find how many boys are in the class.", sol:"Boys: 25 − 10 = 15. Probability {15|25} = {3|5}. Answer: {3|5}.", reduced:true},
    {q:"A pie chart shows how Misha spends his day (24 hours). The &ldquo;sleep&rdquo; slice has an angle of 135°. How many hours a day does Misha sleep?", ans:"9", hint:"What share of 360° is 135°?", sol:"{135|360} = {3|8}. {3|8} of 24 h: 24 : 8 · 3 = 9 h. Answer: 9."},
    {q:"Vika was looking for the median of the set 7, 2, 10, 4, 9, 1. She took two numbers from the middle of the list: (10 + 4) : 2 = 7. That's wrong. Find the correct median.", ans:"5.5", hint:"Before finding the median, the numbers need to be written in increasing order.", sol:"In order: 1, 2, 4, 7, 9, 10. The middle numbers are 4 and 7: (4 + 7) : 2 = 5.5. Answer: 5.5."},
    {q:"A cyclist rode 12 km in 40 minutes, then rested for 20 minutes, then rode another 18 km in 1 hour. Find his average speed over the whole trip, including the rest (in km/h).", ans:"15", hint:"Average speed = whole distance : whole time. The rest time is also part of the whole time.", sol:"Distance 12 + 18 = 30 km. Time 40 + 20 + 60 = 120 min = 2 h. 30 : 2 = 15 km/h. Answer: 15."},
    {q:"The average height of five basketball players is 190 cm. A rookie joined the team, and the average height of the six players became 191 cm. How tall is the rookie (in cm)?", ans:"196", hint:"Find the sum of heights before and after the rookie joined.", sol:"Before: 190 · 5 = 950 cm. After: 191 · 6 = 1146 cm. Rookie: 1146 − 950 = 196 cm. Answer: 196."},
    {q:"In the set 3, 8, 5, x, 8, 12, the number x is the smallest, and the range of the set is 11. Find x.", ans:"1", hint:"The range is the largest number minus the smallest. The largest here is 12.", sol:"12 − x = 11, so x = 1. Check: 1 is less than all the other numbers, range 12 − 1 = 11. Answer: 1."},
    {q:"A number from 1 to 20 is chosen at random. Find the probability that it's divisible by 3 (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"3/10", hint:"List the numbers up to 20 that are divisible by 3.", sol:"These fit: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18 — 6 numbers out of 20. {6|20} = {3|10}. Answer: {3|10}.", reduced:true}
  ],
  bonus: {q:"The arithmetic mean of five different natural numbers is 6. What's the largest value that the greatest of these numbers can take?", ans:"20", hint:"The sum of all five numbers is known. For one number to be as large as possible, the other four need to be as small as possible (but they must all be different).", sol:"Sum of the numbers 6 · 5 = 30. The four smallest different natural numbers are 1, 2, 3, 4, with a sum of 10. Then the fifth number is at most 30 − 10 = 20. Example: 1, 2, 3, 4, 20 — mean (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 20) : 5 = 6."},
  test: [
    {q:"Find the arithmetic mean of the numbers 15, 22, 9 and 30.", ans:"19", sol:"(15 + 22 + 9 + 30) : 4 = 76 : 4 = 19."},
    {q:"Find the median of the set: 14, 3, 9, 21, 7.", ans:"9", sol:"Ordered: 3, 7, 9, 14, 21. The middle is 9."},
    {q:"Find the range of the set: 17, 4, 22, 9.", ans:"18", sol:"The largest is 22, the smallest is 4. Range 22 − 4 = 18."},
    {q:"2 kg of cheese at 60 rubles per kilogram and 3 kg of sausage at 80 rubles per kilogram were bought. Find the average price per kilogram (in rubles).", ans:"72", sol:"Cost: 2 · 60 + 3 · 80 = 120 + 240 = 360 rubles for 5 kg. Average price 360 : 5 = 72 rubles."},
    {q:"The arithmetic mean of six numbers is 8. The number 15 was added to them. Find the arithmetic mean of the seven numbers.", ans:"9", sol:"Sum of six: 8 · 6 = 48. With the added number: 48 + 15 = 63. Mean 63 : 7 = 9."},
    {q:"There are 4 white and 6 black balls in a bag. Find the probability of drawing a white ball (give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms).", ans:"2/5", reduced:true, sol:"There are 10 balls in total, 4 favorable. Probability 4/10 = 2/5."},
    {q:"A cyclist rode for 1 hour at 45 km/h and for 2 hours at 60 km/h. Find his average speed (in km/h).", ans:"55", sol:"Distance: 45 · 1 + 60 · 2 = 45 + 120 = 165 km. Time 3 h. Speed 165 : 3 = 55 km/h."},
    {q:"150 people took part in a survey. On the pie chart, the &ldquo;chess&rdquo; slice has an angle of 72°. How many people chose chess?", ans:"30", sol:"72° : 360° = 1/5. One fifth of 150 is 30 people."}
  ],
  lab: [
    {t:"Average height", b:"Measure the height of five people (family or friends) and find the arithmetic mean, the range and the median. Do the mean and the median match? Add a sixth person to the list — for example, an adult basketball player 200 cm tall (you can make this up). What happened to the mean, and what happened to the median? Write down your conclusion."},
    {t:"Rolling a die", b:"Roll a die 60 times and record the results in a frequency table (how many times 1, 2, …, 6 came up). Build a bar chart. What's the mode? Compare the frequency of, say, sixes (number of times it came up : 60) with the probability 1/6. They're close, but probably not equal — repeat the experiment with more rolls."},
    {t:"Average speed along a route", b:"Walk the same distance (for example, 100 steps, or a stretch along your house) there and back, timing yourself with a stopwatch: go fast one way, slow the other. Find the average speed as the whole distance divided by the whole time, and compare it to the average of the two speeds. Which one turned out closer to the truth, and why?"},
    {t:"A pie chart made of candy", b:"Take a bag of multicolored candy or buttons and sort them by color. Count how many of each color there are, and find the share of each color out of the total. Convert the shares into degrees (share · 360°) and draw a pie chart using a protractor. Check that the sum of all the angles equals 360°."}
  ],
  steps: [
    {title:"Why we need statistics", body:"<p>When you need to quickly understand what's going on with a set of numbers, you don't look through every single one — you find one number that describes the whole set: &ldquo;the average grade,&rdquo; &ldquo;the average temperature,&rdquo; &ldquo;the typical price.&rdquo; That's what statistics does. Its close friend is probability: it measures how likely an event is.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_MEAN_ANIM__</div>"},
    {title:"Arithmetic mean", body:"<h3>Arithmetic mean</h3><p>Watch how the blocks even out into equal columns — that's how you find the <b>arithmetic mean</b>.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_MEAN_ANIM__</div><div class='note'><span class='hand'>Remember</span>Arithmetic mean = sum of all the numbers : the number of numbers. The mean always lies between the smallest and the largest number in the set.</div><p>Some useful things follow from the formula. If you know the mean and the number of values, then <b>sum = mean · count</b>.</p>", exIdx:0},
    {title:"Range, mode, median", body:"<h3>Range, mode, median</h3><p>Take the set: 5, 2, 8, 5, 3, 9, 5. The <b>range</b> is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers: 9 − 2 = 7. It shows how spread out the numbers are. The <b>mode</b> is the number that appears most often in the set: here it's 5 (three times). The <b>median</b> is the number in the middle of the set once it's written in increasing order. If there's an even number of values, the median equals the arithmetic mean of the two middle numbers.</p><div class='warn'><span class='hand'>Common mistake</span>Looking for the median without writing the numbers in order first. Always order the set first! Another mistake is thinking the range and the mean are the same thing.</div>", exIdx:1},
    {title:"Mode in practice", body:"<p>To find the mode, count how many times each number appears in the set, and pick the one that appears most often.</p>", exIdx:2},
    {title:"Tables and charts", body:"<h3>Tables and charts</h3><p>Data is written into a table or drawn as a picture. In a <b>bar chart</b>, the height of a bar equals its value, which makes comparing easy. In a <b>pie chart</b>, the whole circle is the total (100%, 360°), and a slice shows a share. The bigger the slice's angle, the bigger the share.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_PIE__</div><p>To find how many people are in a slice, find that share of the total number.</p>", exIdx:3},
    {title:"Average price", body:"<h3>Average price and average speed</h3><p>Say you bought several kilograms of one item at one price, and several kilograms of another item at a different price. The average price isn't the sum of the prices divided by two! We work out the total cost and divide by the total weight. Rule: <b>average value = &ldquo;total&rdquo; : &ldquo;total count&rdquo;</b>.</p>", exIdx:4},
    {title:"Average speed", body:"<p>It works the same way with speed: <b>average speed = whole distance : whole time</b>, not the sum of the speeds divided by two.</p>", exIdx:5},
    {title:"How the mean changes", body:"<h3>How the mean changes</h3><p>If you add a number <i>greater</i> than the mean to the set, the mean goes up; if it's <i>smaller</i>, the mean goes down. We work it out through sums: find the old sum (mean · count), add the new number, divide by the new count of numbers.</p>", exIdx:6},
    {title:"Probability", body:"<h3>Probability</h3><p>In simple cases, where all outcomes are equally likely, <b>the probability of an event = the number of favorable outcomes : the total number of outcomes</b>. Probability is always between 0 (an impossible event) and 1 (a certain one). For two dice, look at the table: there are 36 outcomes in total.</p><div class='figure'>__SVG_STAT6_DICE__</div><p>A sum of 7 comes up in 6 cells, so the probability is {6|36} = {1|6}. It's standard to reduce the fraction.</p>", exIdx:7}
  ]
};


